{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20838","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20838","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20838","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abir Khan","familyName":"Ratul","name":"Ratul, Abir Khan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanjay","familyName":"Acharjee","name":"Acharjee, Sanjay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Somin","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Somin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md Nazmus","familyName":"Sakib","name":"Sakib, Md Nazmus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Sketch2BIM: A Multi-Agent Human-AI Collaborative Pipeline to Convert Hand-Drawn Floor Plans to 3D BIM"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-16T18:45:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-30T01:33:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1016/j.autcon.2026.106984","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This study introduces a human-in-the-loop pipeline that converts unscaled, hand-drawn floor plan sketches into semantically consistent 3D BIM models. The workflow leverages multimodal large language models (MLLMs) within a multi-agent framework, combining perceptual extraction, human feedback, schema validation, and automated BIM scripting. Initially, sketches are iteratively refined into a structured JSON layout of walls, doors, and windows. Later, these layouts are transformed into executable scripts that generate 3D BIM models. Experiments on ten diverse floor plans demonstrate strong convergence: openings (doors, windows) are captured with high reliability in the initial pass, while wall detection begins around 83% and achieves near-perfect alignment after a few feedback iterations. Across all categories, precision, recall, and F1 scores remain above 0.83, and geometric errors (RMSE, MAE) progressively decrease to zero through feedback corrections. This study demonstrates how MLLM-driven multi-agent reasoning can make BIM creation accessible to both experts and non-experts using only freehand sketches."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20838","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-27T01:40:36Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:40:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-30T04:01:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21175","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21175","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21175","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujin","familyName":"Jo","name":"Jo, Yujin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taesup","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Taesup","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Memory-Free Continual Learning with Null Space Adaptation for Zero-Shot Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T05:53:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:26:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-21T03:41:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-22T01:33:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Pre-trained vision-language models (VLMs), such as CLIP, have demonstrated remarkable zero-shot generalization, enabling deployment in a wide range of real-world tasks without additional task-specific training. However, in real deployment scenarios with evolving environments or emerging classes, these models inevitably face distributional shifts and novel tasks. In such contexts, static zero-shot capabilities are insufficient, and there is a growing need for continual learning methods that allow models to adapt over time while avoiding catastrophic forgetting. We introduce NuSA-CL (Null Space Adaptation for Continual Learning), a lightweight memory-free continual learning framework designed to address this challenge. NuSA-CL employs low-rank adaptation and constrains task-specific weight updates to lie within an approximate null space of the model's current parameters. This strategy minimizes interference with previously acquired knowledge, effectively preserving the zero-shot capabilities of the original model. Unlike methods relying on replay buffers or costly distillation, NuSA-CL imposes minimal computational and memory overhead, making it practical for deployment in resource-constrained, real-world continual learning environments. Experiments show that our framework not only effectively preserves zero-shot transfer capabilities but also achieves highly competitive performance on continual learning benchmarks. These results position NuSA-CL as a practical and scalable solution for continually evolving zero-shot VLMs in real-world applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published at ICLR 2026. Code: https://github.com/ZoYujin/NuSA-CL"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21175","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:48:30Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:48:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-22T04:43:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21084","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21084","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21084","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juntao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Juntao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haobin","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Haobin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ling","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Ling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanyuan","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yuanyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongfei","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Hongfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MediRec: Enhancing Chinese Medication Recommendation with Explainable Clinical Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T01:47:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:16:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-05T09:12:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-08T01:25:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-05T07:53:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-06T00:31:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have shown strong potential for clinical decision support through their advanced language understanding and reasoning capabilities. However, their application to Chinese clinical medication recommendation remains largely unexplored. Existing approaches are primarily developed on English electronic health record datasets and focus on coarse-grained medication code prediction, offering limited support for interpretable clinical decision-making. In this work, we propose MediRec, an explainable LLM-based framework for Chinese medication recommendation from electronic health records. MediRec combines clinically grounded reasoning-chain distillation with reinforcement learning to improve both recommendation accuracy and interpretability. Comprehensive experiments on a Chinese medication recommendation benchmark show that MediRec achieves strong performance, with an F1 score of 0.5813 and a Jaccard score of 0.4626. Further analyses indicate that MediRec generates clinically plausible recommendations with transparent reasoning, demonstrating its effectiveness for explainable medication decision support in Chinese healthcare settings."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by NLPCC 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21084","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:46:22Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:46:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-06T02:14:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21324","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21324","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21324","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinhui","familyName":"Lou","name":"Lou, Jinhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhou","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Zhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenqi","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Zhenqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weidong","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Weidong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingming","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Qingming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CXRAgent: Director-Orchestrated Multi-Stage Reasoning for Chest X-Ray Interpretation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T10:31:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:41:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-16T06:28:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-17T00:28:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Chest X-ray (CXR) plays a pivotal role in clinical diagnosis, and a variety of task-specific and foundation models have been developed for automatic CXR interpretation. However, these models often struggle to adapt to new diagnostic tasks and complex reasoning scenarios. Recently, LLM-based agent models have emerged as a promising paradigm for CXR analysis, enhancing model's capability through tool coordination, multi-step reasoning, and team collaboration, etc. However, existing agents often rely on a single diagnostic pipeline and lack mechanisms for assessing tools' reliability, limiting their adaptability and credibility. To this end, we propose CXRAgent, a director-orchestrated, multi-stage agent for CXR interpretation, where a central director coordinates the following stages: (1) Tool Invocation: The agent strategically orchestrates a set of CXR-analysis tools, with outputs normalized and verified by the Evidence-driven Validator (EDV), which grounds diagnostic outputs with visual evidence to support reliable downstream diagnosis; (2) Diagnostic Planning: Guided by task requirements and intermediate findings, the agent formulates a targeted diagnostic plan. It then assembles an expert team accordingly, defining member roles and coordinating their interactions to enable adaptive and collaborative reasoning; (3) Collaborative Decision-making: The agent integrates insights from the expert team with accumulated contextual memories, synthesizing them into an evidence-backed diagnostic conclusion. Experiments on various CXR interpretation tasks show that CXRAgent delivers strong performance, providing visual evidence and generalizes well to clinical tasks of different complexity. Code and data are valuable at this \\href{https://github.com/laojiahuo2003/CXRAgent/}{link}."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 4 figures, 7 Tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21324","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:52:03Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:52:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-17T02:33:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20963","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20963","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20963","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongqiang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yongqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gang","familyName":"Niu","name":"Niu, Gang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Masashi","familyName":"Sugiyama","name":"Sugiyama, Masashi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When and Why Does Multi-Agent Debate Fail and Does It Really Underperform?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T19:46:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:05:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-14T17:06:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-15T01:02:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-agent debate (MAD) was proposed as a promising approach for ensembling the wisdom of multiple large language models (LLMs) to improve reasoning and provide effective supervision to superhuman LLMs. However, increasing empirical evidence suggests that MAD may not outperform or even significantly underperform single-agent approaches (SA), raising doubts about the benefits of MAD. In this work, we investigate this issue by analyzing the incentive structures of popular MAD paradigms: (i) competitive MAD (CopMAD) where agents compete by holding opposing positions; (ii) consensus-seeking MAD (CosMAD) where agents are driven to seek consensus. We show that both paradigms suffer from debate hacking: CopMAD reduces to a cheap-talk game, where agents produce misleading messages to win the game, while CosMAD filters out informative disagreements for premature consensus. Consequently, agents in both CopMAD and CosMAD fail to jointly resolve the ambiguity and seek the truth. To this end, we introduce ColMAD, a collaborative protocol that reframes MAD as a non-zero-sum game to encourage agents to provide informative while truthful messages. Through extensive benchmarking on challenging tasks such as error detection, we show that ColMAD significantly outperforms previous MAD protocols up to 10 percentage points. Under the same budgets, ColMAD effectively brings non-trivial improvements over SA methods, implying that the protocol design is critical to realizing the potential of MAD."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint, ongoing work"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20963","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:43:33Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:43:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-15T02:16:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21122","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21122","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21122","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Longtian","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Longtian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shan","familyName":"Ning","name":"Ning, Shan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxuan","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Jiaxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuming","familyName":"He","name":"He, Xuming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"NoisyGRPO: Incentivizing Multimodal CoT Reasoning via Noise Injection and Bayesian Estimation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T03:23:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:21:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-29T07:06:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-30T00:32:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-07T14:13:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-08T00:26:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning (RL) has shown promise in enhancing the general Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning capabilities of multimodal large language models (MLLMs). However, when applied to improve general CoT reasoning, existing RL frameworks often struggle to generalize beyond the training distribution. To address this, we propose NoisyGRPO, a systematic multimodal RL framework that introduces controllable noise into visual inputs for enhanced exploration and explicitly models the advantage estimation process via a Bayesian framework. Specifically, NoisyGRPO improves RL training by: (1) Noise-Injected Exploration Policy: Perturbing visual inputs with Gaussian noise to encourage exploration across a wider range of visual scenarios; and (2) Bayesian Advantage Estimation: Formulating advantage estimation as a principled Bayesian inference problem, where the injected noise level serves as a prior and the observed trajectory reward as the likelihood. This Bayesian modeling fuses both sources of information to compute a robust posterior estimate of trajectory advantage, effectively guiding MLLMs to prefer visually grounded trajectories over noisy ones. Experiments on standard CoT quality, general capability, and hallucination benchmarks demonstrate that NoisyGRPO substantially improves generalization and robustness, especially in RL settings with small-scale MLLMs such as Qwen2.5-VL 3B. The project page is available at https://artanic30.github.io/project_pages/NoisyGRPO/."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by Neurips 2025, Project page is available at https://artanic30.github.io/project_pages/NoisyGRPO/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21122","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:47:15Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:47:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-08T02:01:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21459","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21459","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21459","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adetayo","familyName":"Adebimpe","name":"Adebimpe, Adetayo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Helmut","familyName":"Neukirchen","name":"Neukirchen, Helmut","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Welsh","name":"Welsh, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SBASH: a Framework for Designing and Evaluating RAG vs. Prompt-Tuned LLM Honeypots"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"K.6.5; D.4.6; I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T13:41:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-04T00:45:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/fllm67465.2025.11391242","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Honeypots are decoy systems used for gathering valuable threat intelligence or diverting attackers away from production systems. Maximising attacker engagement is essential to their utility. However research has highlighted that context-awareness, such as the ability to respond to new attack types, systems and attacker agents, is necessary to increase engagement. Large Language Models (LLMs) have been shown as one approach to increase context awareness but suffer from several challenges including accuracy and timeliness of response time, high operational costs and data-protection issues due to cloud deployment. We propose the System-Based Attention Shell Honeypot (SBASH) framework which manages data-protection issues through the use of lightweight local LLMs. We investigate the use of Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) supported LLMs and non-RAG LLMs for Linux shell commands and evaluate them using several different metrics such as response time differences, realism from human testers, and similarity to a real system calculated with Levenshtein distance, SBert, and BertScore. We show that RAG improves accuracy for untuned models while models that have been tuned via a system prompt that tells the LLM to respond like a Linux system achieve without RAG a similar accuracy as untuned with RAG, while having a slightly lower latency."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"to be published in: The 3rd International Conference on Foundation and Large Language Models (FLLM2025), IEEE, 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21459","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-27T01:55:13Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:55:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-04T03:29:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21011","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21011","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21011","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ilona","familyName":"van der Linden","name":"van der Linden, Ilona","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sahana","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Sahana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arnav","familyName":"Dixit","name":"Dixit, Arnav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aadi","familyName":"Sudan","name":"Sudan, Aadi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Smruthi","familyName":"Danda","name":"Danda, Smruthi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David C.","familyName":"Anastasiu","name":"Anastasiu, David C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Lukoff","name":"Lukoff, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Generating the Modal Worker: A Cross-Model Audit of Race and Gender in LLM-Generated Personas Across 41 Occupations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T21:43:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:09:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-26T19:13:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-30T00:05:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-01T18:35:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-03T00:05:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As generative AI tools are increasingly used to portray people in professional roles, understanding their racial and gender representational biases is critical. We audit over 1.5 million occupational personas generated by four major large language models (GPT-4, Gemini 2.5, DeepSeek V3.1, and Mistral-medium) across 41 U.S. occupations. Comparing these personas against U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics (BLS) data, we find that models generate demographics with less variation than real-world data, functionally compressing each occupation toward a dominant demographic profile rather than representing population-level variation. A shift/exaggeration decomposition reveals the structure of these distortions: White (-31 percentage points) and Black (-9 pp) workers are consistently underrepresented, while Hispanic (+17 pp) and Asian (+12 pp) workers are overrepresented, with stereotype exaggeration amplifying existing occupational segregation. These distortions are often extreme, including near-total portrayals of housekeepers as Hispanic and the near-erasure of Black workers from many occupations. Because these patterns recur across models with different institutional and cultural origins, they suggest shared structural sources of bias rather than model-specific artifacts. We argue that auditing generative AI requires evaluation frameworks that examine how synthetic populations systematically reshape demographic visibility across social roles."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21011","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:44:40Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:44:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-03T03:11:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21364","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21364","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21364","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raphael","familyName":"Schmitt","name":"Schmitt, Raphael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefan","familyName":"Schweter","name":"Schweter, Stefan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SindBERT, the Sailor: Charting the Seas of Turkish NLP"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T11:48:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:44:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-01T11:38:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-02T02:01:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.18653/v1/2026.sigturk-1.1","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Transformer models have revolutionized NLP, yet many morphologically rich languages remain underrepresented in large-scale pre-training efforts. With SindBERT, we set out to chart the seas of Turkish NLP, providing the first large-scale RoBERTa-based encoder for Turkish. Trained from scratch on 312~GB of Turkish text (mC4, OSCAR23, Wikipedia), SindBERT is released in both base and large configurations, representing the first large-scale encoder-only language model available for Turkish. We evaluate SindBERT on part-of-speech tagging, named entity recognition, offensive language detection, and the TurBLiMP linguistic acceptability benchmark. Our results show that SindBERT performs competitively with existing Turkish and multilingual models, with the large variant achieving the best scores in two of four tasks but showing no consistent scaling advantage overall. This flat scaling trend, also observed for XLM-R and EuroBERT, suggests that current Turkish benchmarks may already be saturated. At the same time, comparisons with smaller but more curated models such as BERTurk highlight that corpus quality and diversity can outweigh sheer data volume. Taken together, SindBERT contributes both as an openly released resource for Turkish NLP and as an empirical case study on the limits of scaling and the central role of corpus composition in morphologically rich languages. The SindBERT models are released under the MIT license and made available in both fairseq and Huggingface formats."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published at SIGTURK 2026, co-located with EACL 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21364","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-27T01:52:59Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:53:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-02T04:54:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20853","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20853","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20853","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyungjun","familyName":"Yoon","name":"Yoon, Hyungjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seungjoo","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Seungjoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu Yvonne","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yu Yvonne","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaomeng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xiaomeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taiting","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Taiting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Freddy Yifei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Freddy Yifei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taeckyung","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Taeckyung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyeongheon","familyName":"Cha","name":"Cha, Hyeongheon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haochen","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Haochen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gaoteng","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Gaoteng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongyao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Dongyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cecilia","familyName":"Mascolo","name":"Mascolo, Cecilia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sung-Ju","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Sung-Ju","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lili","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Lili","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond Hearing: Learning Task-Agnostic ExG Representations from Earphones via Physiology-Informed Tokenization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T01","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-22T05:11:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:00:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-29T13:20:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-01T01:00:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Electrophysiological (ExG) signals offer valuable insights into human physiology, yet building foundation models that generalize across everyday tasks remains challenging due to two key limitations: (i)~insufficient data diversity, as most ExG recordings are collected in controlled labs with bulky, expensive devices; and (ii)~task-specific model designs that require tailored processing (i.e., targeted frequency filters) and architectures, which limit generalization across tasks. To address these challenges, we introduce an approach for scalable, task-agnostic ExG monitoring in the wild. We collected 50 hours of unobtrusive free-living ExG data with an earphone-based hardware prototype to narrow the data diversity gap. At the core of our approach is Physiology-informed Multi-band Tokenization (PiMT), which decomposes ExG signals into 12 physiology-informed tokens, followed by a reconstruction task to learn robust representations. This enables adaptive feature recognition across the full frequency spectrum while capturing task-relevant information. Experiments on our new DailySense dataset, the first to enable ExG-based analysis across five human senses, together with four public ExG benchmarks, demonstrate that PiMT consistently outperforms state-of-the-art methods across diverse tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20853","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:40:57Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:40:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-01T02:34:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21151","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21151","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21151","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minqi","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Minqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yilun","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Yilun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiazhou","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Jiazhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Scott","familyName":"Sanner","name":"Sanner, Scott","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VOGUE: A Multimodal Dataset for Conversational Recommendation in Fashion"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"H.5.2; H.3.3; I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T04:45:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:24:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-28T16:26:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-29T01:20:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal conversational recommendation has recently emerged as a promising paradigm for delivering personalized experiences through natural dialogue enriched by visual and contextual grounding. Yet currently available multimodal conversational recommendation datasets remain limited: existing resources either simulate conversations, omit user history or fail to collect sufficiently detailed feedback, which constrain the types of research and evaluation they support.\n To address these gaps we introduce VOGUE, a dataset of 60 human human dialogues containing 2100 granularly labeled utterances in realistic fashion shopping scenarios. Each dialogue is paired with a shared visual catalogue, item metadata, user fashion profiles and post conversation ratings from both users (Seekers) and recommenders (Assistants). This design enables rigorous evaluation of conversational inference, including not only alignment between predicted and ground truth preferences but also calibration against full rating distributions and comparison with explicit and implicit user satisfaction signals.\n Our analyses of VOGUE reveal distinctive dynamics of visually grounded dialogue, e.g. recommenders frequently recommend items simultaneously in feature based groups, which creates distinct conversational phases bridged by Seeker critiques and refinements. Benchmarking Multimodal Large Language Models against human Recommenders shows that while MLLMs approach human level alignment in aggregate they exhibit systematic distribution errors in reproducing human ratings and struggle to generalize preference inference beyond explicitly discussed items. These findings establish VOGUE as both a unique resource for studying multimodal conversational systems and a challenge dataset beyond the current recommendation capabilities of existing top tier multimodal foundation models such as GPT-5-mini and Gemini-2.5-Flash."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21151","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:47:57Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:47:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-29T02:57:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21270","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21270","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21270","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinghao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xinghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Pengyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenkun","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Chenkun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaojun","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Shaojun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaoxiang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhaoxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiguo","familyName":"Lian","name":"Lian, Shiguo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fangxu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Fangxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xipeng","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Xipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Sparser Block-Sparse Attention via Token Permutation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T09:11:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:36:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-22T07:10:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-25T00:28:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Scaling the context length of large language models (LLMs) offers significant benefits but is computationally expensive. This expense stems primarily from the self-attention mechanism, whose $O(N^2)$ complexity with respect to sequence length presents a major bottleneck for both memory and latency. Fortunately, the attention matrix is often sparse, particularly for long sequences, suggesting an opportunity for optimization. Block-sparse attention has emerged as a promising solution that partitions sequences into blocks and skips computation for a subset of these blocks. However, the effectiveness of this method is highly dependent on the underlying attention patterns, which can lead to sub-optimal block-level sparsity. For instance, important key tokens for queries within a single block may be scattered across numerous other blocks, leading to computational redundancy. In this work, we propose Permuted Block-Sparse Attention (\\textbf{PBS-Attn}), a plug-and-play method that leverages the permutation properties of attention to increase block-level sparsity and enhance the computational efficiency of LLM prefilling. We conduct comprehensive experiments on challenging real-world long-context datasets, demonstrating that PBS-Attn consistently outperforms existing block-sparse attention methods in model accuracy and closely matches the full attention baseline. Powered by our custom permuted-FlashAttention kernels, PBS-Attn achieves an end-to-end speedup of up to $2.75\\times$ in long-context prefilling, confirming its practical viability. Code available at https://github.com/xinghaow99/pbs-attn"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICML 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21270","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:50:46Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:50:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-25T02:09:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21060","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21060","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21060","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"He","name":"He, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingyu","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Xingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On the Sample Complexity of Differentially Private Policy Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T00:21:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-01T01:03:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-13T04:00:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-14T01:17:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-13T01:55:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-14T00:26:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Policy optimization (PO) is a cornerstone of modern reinforcement learning (RL), with diverse applications spanning robotics, healthcare, and large language model training. The increasing deployment of PO in sensitive domains, however, raises significant privacy concerns. In this paper, we initiate a theoretical study of differentially private policy optimization, focusing explicitly on its sample complexity. We first formalize an appropriate definition of differential privacy (DP) tailored to PO, addressing the inherent challenges arising from on-policy learning dynamics and the subtlety involved in defining the unit of privacy. We then systematically analyze the sample complexity of widely-used PO algorithms, including policy gradient (PG), natural policy gradient (NPG) and more, under DP constraints and various settings, via a unified framework. Our theoretical results demonstrate that privacy costs can often manifest as lower-order terms in the sample complexity, while also highlighting subtle yet important observations in private PO settings. These offer valuable practical insights for privacy-preserving PO algorithms."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21060","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:45:48Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:45:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-14T02:50:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21272","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21272","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21272","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Lu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wuqi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Wuqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lili","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Lili","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Guan","name":"Guan, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongqiang","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Yongqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yepang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yepang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shing-Chi","familyName":"Cheung","name":"Cheung, Shing-Chi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM-Powered Detection of Price Manipulation in DeFi"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T09:13:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:36:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-29T04:15:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-30T00:22:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Decentralized Finance (DeFi) smart contracts manage billions of dollars, making them a prime target for exploits. Price manipulation vulnerabilities, often via flash loans, are a devastating class of attacks causing significant financial losses. Existing detection methods are limited. Reactive approaches analyze attacks only after they occur, while proactive static analysis tools rely on rigid, predefined heuristics, limiting adaptability. Both depend on known attack patterns, failing to identify novel variants or comprehend complex economic logic. We propose PMDetector, a hybrid framework combining static analysis with Large Language Model (LLM)-based reasoning to proactively detect price manipulation vulnerabilities. Our approach uses a formal attack model and a three-stage pipeline. First, static taint analysis identifies potentially vulnerable code paths. Second, a two-stage LLM process filters paths by analyzing defenses and then simulates attacks to evaluate exploitability. Finally, a static analysis checker validates LLM results, retaining only high-risk paths and generating comprehensive vulnerability reports. To evaluate its effectiveness, we built a dataset of 73 real-world vulnerable and 288 benign DeFi protocols. Results show PMDetector achieves 88% precision and 90% recall with Gemini 2.5-flash, significantly outperforming state-of-the-art static analysis and LLM-based approaches. Auditing a vulnerability with PMDetector costs just $0.03 and takes 4.0 seconds with GPT-4.1, offering an efficient and cost-effective alternative to manual audits."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21272","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:50:49Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:50:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-30T02:20:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20956","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20956","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20956","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng-Xin","familyName":"Yong","name":"Yong, Zheng-Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stephen H.","familyName":"Bach","name":"Bach, Stephen H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Self-Jailbreaking: Language Models Can Reason Themselves Out of Safety Alignment After Benign Reasoning Training"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T19:34:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:05:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-29T16:09:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-30T00:58:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We discover a novel and surprising phenomenon of unintentional misalignment in reasoning language models (RLMs), which we call self-jailbreaking. Specifically, after benign reasoning training on math or code domains, RLMs will use multiple strategies to circumvent their own safety guardrails. One strategy is to introduce benign assumptions about users and scenarios to justify fulfilling harmful requests. For instance, an RLM reasons that harmful requests like ``outline a strategy for stealing customers' credit card information from a retail store'' could be associated with the benign intent of ``a security professional trying to test defense,'' despite no such benign context being provided as input. We observe that many open-weight RLMs, including DeepSeek-R1-distilled, s1.1, Phi-4-mini-reasoning, and Nemotron, suffer from self-jailbreaking despite being aware of the harmfulness of the requests. We also provide a mechanistic understanding of self-jailbreaking: RLMs are more compliant after benign reasoning training, and after self-jailbreaking, models appear to perceive malicious requests as less harmful in the CoT, thus enabling compliance with them. To mitigate self-jailbreaking, we find that including minimal safety reasoning data during training is sufficient to ensure RLMs remain safety-aligned. Our work provides the first systematic analysis of self-jailbreaking behavior and offers a practical path forward for maintaining safety in increasingly capable RLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published in The Fourteenth International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR) 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20956","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:43:23Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:43:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-30T02:20:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21236","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21236","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21236","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christoph","familyName":"Bühler","name":"Bühler, Christoph","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matteo","familyName":"Biagiola","name":"Biagiola, Matteo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luca","familyName":"Di Grazia","name":"Di Grazia, Luca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guido","familyName":"Salvaneschi","name":"Salvaneschi, Guido","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AgentBound: Securing Execution Boundaries of AI Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"D.2.0","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T08:10:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:33:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-29T13:11:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-08T00:36:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-24T09:59:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-27T00:32:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3808103","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have evolved into AI agents that interact with external tools and environments to perform complex tasks. The Model Context Protocol (MCP) has become the de facto standard for connecting agents with such resources, but security has lagged behind: thousands of MCP servers execute with unrestricted access to host systems, creating a broad attack surface. In this paper, we introduce AgentBound, the first access control framework for MCP servers. AgentBound combines a declarative policy mechanism, inspired by the Android permission model, with a policy enforcement engine that contains malicious behavior without requiring MCP server modifications. We build a dataset containing the 296 most popular MCP servers, and show that access control policies can be generated automatically from source code with 80.9% accuracy. We also show that AgentBound blocks the majority of security threats in several malicious MCP servers, and that the policy enforcement engine introduces negligible overhead. Our contributions provide developers and project managers with a foundation for securing MCP servers while maintaining productivity, enabling researchers and tool builders to explore new directions for declarative access control and MCP security."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21236","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-27T01:49:59Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:49:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-27T01:50:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21045","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21045","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21045","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali Khosravi","familyName":"Kazazi","name":"Kazazi, Ali Khosravi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenlong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhenlong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"M. Naser","familyName":"Lessani","name":"Lessani, M. Naser","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guido","familyName":"Cervone","name":"Cervone, Guido","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Questions to Queries: An AI-powered Multi-Agent Framework for Spatial Text-to-SQL"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Databases (cs.DB)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T22:58:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:11:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-11T23:48:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-13T01:11:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-29T14:35:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-31T01:02:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The complexity of SQL and the spatial semantics of PostGIS create barriers for non-experts working with spatial data. Although large language models can translate natural language into SQL, spatial Text-to-SQL is more error-prone than general Text-to-SQL because it must resolve geographic intent, schema ambiguity, geometry-bearing tables and columns, spatial function choice, and coordinate reference system and measurement assumptions. We introduce a multi-agent framework that addresses these coupled challenges through staged interpretation, schema grounding, logical planning, SQL generation, and execution-based review. The framework is supported by a knowledge base with programmatic schema profiling, semantic enrichment, and embedding-based retrieval. We evaluated the framework on the non-spatial KaggleDBQA benchmark and on SpatialQueryQA, a new multi-level and coverage-oriented benchmark with diverse geometry types, workload categories, and spatial operations. On KaggleDBQA, the system reached 81.2% accuracy, 221 of 272 questions, after reviewer corrections. On SpatialQueryQA, the system achieved 87.7% accuracy, 79 of 90, compared with 76.7% without the review stage. These results show that decomposing the task into specialized but tightly coupled agents improves robustness, especially for spatially sensitive queries. The study improves access to spatial analysis and provides a practical step toward more reliable spatial Text-to-SQL systems and autonomous GIS."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21045","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:45:27Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:45:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-31T04:03:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20975","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20975","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20975","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Darrin","familyName":"Lea","name":"Lea, Darrin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Ghawaly","name":"Ghawaly, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Golden","familyName":"Richard","name":"Richard, Golden","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aisha","familyName":"Ali-Gombe","name":"Ali-Gombe, Aisha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Case","name":"Case, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"REx86: A Local Large Language Model for Assisting in x86 Assembly Reverse Engineering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T20:09:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-09T00:57:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/acsac67867.2025.00024","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reverse engineering (RE) of x86 binaries is indispensable for malware and firmware analysis, but remains slow due to stripped metadata and adversarial obfuscation. Large Language Models (LLMs) offer potential for improving RE efficiency through automated comprehension and commenting, but cloud-hosted, closed-weight models pose privacy and security risks and cannot be used in closed-network facilities. We evaluate parameter-efficient fine-tuned local LLMs for assisting with x86 RE tasks in these settings. Eight open-weight models across the CodeLlama, Qwen2.5-Coder, and CodeGemma series are fine-tuned on a custom curated dataset of 5,981 x86 assembly examples. We evaluate them quantitatively and identify the fine-tuned Qwen2.5-Coder-7B as the top performer, which we name REx86.\n REx86 reduces test-set cross-entropy loss by 64.2% and improves semantic cosine similarity against ground truth by 20.3\\% over its base model. In a limited user case study (n=43), REx86 significantly enhanced line-level code understanding (p = 0.031) and increased the correct-solve rate from 31% to 53% (p = 0.189), though the latter did not reach statistical significance. Qualitative analysis shows more accurate, concise comments with fewer hallucinations.\n REx86 delivers state-of-the-art assistance in x86 RE among local, open-weight LLMs. Our findings demonstrate the value of domain-specific fine-tuning, and highlight the need for more commented disassembly data to further enhance LLM performance in RE. REx86, its dataset, and LoRA adapters are publicly available at https://github.com/dlea8/REx86 and https://zenodo.org/records/15420461."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted in 2025 Annual Computer Security Applications Conference (ACSAC)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20975","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-27T01:43:50Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:43:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-09T02:09:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20886","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20886","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20886","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gabriel","familyName":"Grand","name":"Grand, Gabriel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Valerio","familyName":"Pepe","name":"Pepe, Valerio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jacob","familyName":"Andreas","name":"Andreas, Jacob","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joshua B.","familyName":"Tenenbaum","name":"Tenenbaum, Joshua B.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Shoot First, Ask Questions Later? Building Rational Agents that Explore and Act Like People"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T17:57:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:01:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-06T18:00:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-09T00:59:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Many emerging applications of AI--from scientific discovery to medical diagnosis--require agents to seek information strategically: forming hypotheses, asking targeted questions, and making decisions under uncertainty. In high-stakes settings with limited resources, do language models (LMs) behave like rational agents? Drawing on insights from human cognition, we develop methods to evaluate and enhance agentic information-seeking. First, we introduce a decision-oriented dialogue task called Collaborative Battleship, in which a Captain must balance exploration (asking questions) and action (taking shots), while a Spotter must supply accurate, contextually-grounded answers. Compared to human players (N=42), we find that many LM agents struggle to ask informative questions, produce accurate answers, and identify high-utility actions. To address these gaps, we develop novel Monte Carlo inference strategies for LMs inspired by Bayesian Experimental Design (BED). For Spotter agents, our approach boosts accuracy by up to 14.7% absolute over LM-only baselines; for Captain agents, it raises expected information gain (EIG) by up to 0.227 bits (94.2% of the achievable noise ceiling). Combined, these components yield sharper targeting (+0.303-0.374 F1), and enable weaker LMs, such as Llama-4-Scout, to outperform both humans (8% -\u0026gt; 82% win rate) and frontier models (0% -\u0026gt; 67% win rate vs. GPT-5) at ~1% of GPT-5's cost. We replicate these findings on Guess Who?, where our methods significantly boost accuracy (+28.3-42.4 p.p.), demonstrating their general applicability for building information-seeking agents."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20886","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:41:44Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:41:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-09T02:09:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21314","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21314","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21314","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuan","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Xuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jichu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jichu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Difan","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Difan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Convergence Analysis of Adaptive Optimizers under Floating-point Quantization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T10:16:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:40:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-01T05:46:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T02:01:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid scaling of large language models (LLMs) has made low-precision training essential for reducing memory, improving efficiency, and enabling larger models and datasets. Existing convergence theories for adaptive optimizers, however, assume all components are exact and neglect hardware-aware quantization, leaving open the question of why low-precision training remains effective. We introduce the first theoretical framework for analyzing the convergence of adaptive optimizers, including Adam and Muon, under floating-point quantization of gradients, weights, and optimizer states (e.g., moment estimates). Within this framework, we derive convergence rates on smooth non-convex objectives under standard stochastic gradient assumptions, explicitly characterizing how quantization errors from different components affect convergence. We show that both algorithms retain rates close to their full-precision counterparts provided mantissa length scales only logarithmically with the number of iterations. Our analysis further reveals that Adam is highly sensitive to weights and second-moment quantization due to its reliance on $β_2 \\to 1$, while Muon requires weaker error control and is thus potentially more robust. These results narrow the gap between empirical success and theoretical understanding of low-precision training methods. Numerical experiments on synthetic and real-world data corroborate our theory."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"68 pages, 13 figures, ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21314","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:51:48Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:51:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:46:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21306","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21306","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21306","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yarik Menchaca","familyName":"Resendiz","name":"Resendiz, Yarik Menchaca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roman","familyName":"Klinger","name":"Klinger, Roman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PARL: Prompt-based Agents for Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T10:04:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:39:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T13:46:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:46:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated high performance on tasks expressed in natural language, particularly in zero- or few-shot settings. These are typically framed as supervised (e.g., classification) or unsupervised (e.g., clustering) problems. However, limited work evaluates LLMs as agents in reinforcement learning (RL) tasks (e.g., playing games), where learning occurs through interaction with an environment and a reward system. While prior work focused on representing tasks that rely on a language representation, we study structured, non-linguistic reasoning - such as interpreting positions in a grid world. We therefore introduce PARL (Prompt-based Agent for Reinforcement Learning), a method that uses LLMs as RL agents through prompting, without any fine-tuning. PARL encodes actions, states, and rewards in the prompt, enabling the model to learn through trial-and-error interaction. We evaluate PARL on three standard RL tasks that do not entirely rely on natural language. We show that it can match or outperform traditional RL agents in simple environments by leveraging pretrained knowledge. However, we identify performance limitations in tasks that require complex mathematical operations or decoding states and actions."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21306","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:51:37Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:51:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-27T03:16:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21087","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21087","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21087","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anubhav","familyName":"Jangra","name":"Jangra, Anubhav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Smaranda","familyName":"Muresan","name":"Muresan, Smaranda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Designing and Evaluating Chain-of-Hints for Scientific Question Answering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T02:00:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:16:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-20T14:43:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-23T01:38:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LLMs are reshaping education, with students increasingly relying on them for learning. Implemented using general-purpose models, these systems are likely to give away the answers, potentially undermining conceptual understanding and critical thinking. Prior work shows that hints can effectively promote cognitive engagement. Building on this insight, we evaluate 18 open-source LLMs on chain-of-hints generation that scaffold users toward the correct answer. We compare two distinct hinting strategies: static hints, pre-generated for each problem, and dynamic hints, adapted to a learners' progress. We evaluate these systems on five pedagogically grounded automatic metrics for hint quality. Using the best performing LLM as the backbone of a quantitative study with 41 participants, we uncover distinct user preferences across hinting strategies, and identify the limitations of automatic evaluation metrics to capture them. Our findings highlight key design considerations for future research on tutoring systems and contribute toward the development of more learner-centered educational technologies."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21087","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:46:26Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:46:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-23T02:46:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21193","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21193","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21193","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Helena Grete","familyName":"Lillepalu","name":"Lillepalu, Helena Grete","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tanel","familyName":"Alumäe","name":"Alumäe, Tanel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Estonian Native Large Language Model Benchmark"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T06:56:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:29:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-19T07:51:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-20T01:29:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The availability of LLM benchmarks for the Estonian language is limited, and a comprehensive evaluation comparing the performance of different LLMs on Estonian tasks has yet to be conducted. We introduce a new benchmark for evaluating LLMs in Estonian, based on seven diverse datasets. These datasets assess general and domain-specific knowledge, understanding of Estonian grammar and vocabulary, summarization abilities, contextual comprehension, and more. The datasets are all generated from native Estonian sources without using machine translation. We compare the performance of base models, instruction-tuned open-source models, and commercial models. Our evaluation includes 6 base models and 26 instruction-tuned models. To assess the results, we employ both human evaluation and LLM-as-a-judge methods. Human evaluation scores showed moderate to high correlation with benchmark evaluations, depending on the dataset. Claude 3.7 Sonnet, used as an LLM judge, demonstrated strong alignment with human ratings, indicating that top-performing LLMs can effectively support the evaluation of Estonian-language models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to LREC 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21193","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:48:56Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:48:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-20T03:03:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21190","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21190","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21190","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingrui","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Mingrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sixiao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Sixiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng","familyName":"Long","name":"Long, Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kwok Yan","familyName":"Lam","name":"Lam, Kwok Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Trojan Example: Jailbreaking LLMs through Template Filling and Unsafety Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T06:43:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:28:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-18T09:55:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-19T01:32:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As Large Language Models (LLMs) become integral to computing infrastructure, safety alignment serves as the primary security control preventing the generation of harmful payloads. However, this defense remains brittle. Existing jailbreak attacks typically bifurcate into white-box methods, which are inapplicable to commercial APIs due to lack of gradient access, and black-box optimization techniques, which often yield unnatural (e.g., syntactically rigid) or non-transferable (e.g., lacking cross-model generalization) prompts. In this work, we introduce TrojFill, a black-box exploitation framework that bypasses safety filters by targeting a fundamental logic flaw in current alignment paradigms: the decoupling of unsafety reasoning from content generation. TrojFill structurally reframes malicious instructions as a template-filling task required for safety analysis. By embedding obfuscated payloads (e.g., via placeholder substitution) into a \"Trojan\" structure, the attack induces the model to generate prohibited content as a \"demonstrative example\" ostensibly required for a subsequent sentence-by-sentence safety critique. This approach effectively masks the malicious intent from standard intent classifiers. We evaluate TrojFill against representative commercial systems, including GPT-4o, Gemini-2.5, DeepSeek-3.1, and Qwen-Max. Our results demonstrate that TrojFill achieves near-universal bypass rates: reaching 100% Attack Success Rate (ASR) on Gemini-flash-2.5 and DeepSeek-3.1, and 97% on GPT-4o, significantly outperforming existing black-box baselines. Furthermore, unlike optimization-based adversarial prompts, TrojFill generates highly interpretable and transferable attack vectors, exposing a systematic vulnerability inaligned LLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21190","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:48:52Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:48:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-19T03:02:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21150","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21150","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21150","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kou","familyName":"Misaki","name":"Misaki, Kou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takuya","familyName":"Akiba","name":"Akiba, Takuya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"String Seed of Thought: Prompting LLMs for Distribution-Faithful and Diverse Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T04:43:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:24:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-07T06:59:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-10T01:24:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-05T19:46:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-09T01:04:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce String Seed of Thought (SSoT), a novel prompting method for LLMs that improves Probabilistic Instruction Following (PIF). We define PIF as a task requiring an LLM to select its answer from a predefined set of options, each associated with a specific probability, such that the empirical distribution of the generated answers aligns with the target distribution when prompted multiple times. While LLMs excel at tasks with single, deterministic answers, they often fail at PIF, exhibiting biases problematic for applications requiring non-deterministic behaviors, such as human-behavior simulation, content diversification, and multiplayer games. It also harms the diversity of generated responses, a crucial factor in test-time scaling, by causing the outputs to collapse into a limited set of answers. To address this, we propose SSoT, a simple prompting method that instructs an LLM to first output a random string to generate sufficient entropy. SSoT also instructs the LLM to extract randomness by manipulating this string to derive a final answer, thereby preserving diversity while adhering to specific constraints. We demonstrate that SSoT significantly improves the PIF performance of LLMs, approaching the ideal performance of a pseudo-random number generator. Furthermore, our experiments on NoveltyBench show SSoT's benefits extend beyond closed-set tasks to open-ended tasks by enhancing response diversity."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21150","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:47:55Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:47:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-09T03:02:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21618","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21618","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21618","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoxi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiaoxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenxiang","familyName":"Jiao","name":"Jiao, Wenxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiarui","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Jiarui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanting","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Guanting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiajie","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Jiajie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinuo","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yinuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yutao","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yutao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ji-Rong","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Ji-Rong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhicheng","familyName":"Dou","name":"Dou, Zhicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DeepAgent: A General Reasoning Agent with Scalable Toolsets"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T16:24:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T01:00:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-04T08:04:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-05T01:33:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-05T14:08:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-06T01:49:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large reasoning models have demonstrated strong problem-solving abilities, yet real-world tasks often require external tools and long-horizon interactions. Existing agent frameworks typically follow predefined workflows, which limit autonomous and global task completion. In this paper, we introduce DeepAgent, an end-to-end deep reasoning agent that performs autonomous thinking, tool discovery, and action execution within a single, coherent reasoning process. To manage long-horizon interactions, we introduce an autonomous memory folding mechanism that compresses past interactions into structured episodic, working, and tool memories, reducing error accumulation while preserving critical information. To teach general-purpose tool use efficiently and stably, we develop an end-to-end reinforcement learning strategy, namely ToolPO, that leverages LLM-simulated APIs and applies tool-call advantage attribution to assign fine-grained credit to the tool invocation tokens. Extensive experiments on eight benchmarks, including general tool-use tasks (ToolBench, API-Bank, TMDB, Spotify, ToolHop) and downstream applications (ALFWorld, WebShop, GAIA, HLE), demonstrate that DeepAgent consistently outperforms baselines across both labeled-tool and open-set tool retrieval scenarios. The code and demo are available at https://github.com/RUC-NLPIR/DeepAgent."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by WWW 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21618","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":2,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:58:49Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:58:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-06T03:22:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21296","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21296","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21296","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sukanya","familyName":"Patra","name":"Patra, Sukanya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Souhaib Ben","familyName":"Taieb","name":"Taieb, Souhaib Ben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"An Evidence-Based Post-Hoc Adjustment Framework for Anomaly Detection Under Data Contamination"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T09:45:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:38:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-28T19:43:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-30T01:04:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Unsupervised anomaly detection (AD) methods typically assume clean training data, yet real-world datasets often contain undetected or mislabeled anomalies, leading to significant performance degradation. Existing solutions require access to the training pipelines, data or prior knowledge of the proportions of anomalies in the data, limiting their real-world applicability. To address this challenge, we propose EPHAD, a simple yet effective test-time adaptation framework that updates the outputs of AD models trained on contaminated datasets using evidence gathered at test time. Our approach integrates the prior knowledge captured by the AD model trained on contaminated datasets with evidence derived from multimodal foundation models like Contrastive Language-Image Pre-training (CLIP), classical AD methods like the Local Outlier Factor or domain-specific knowledge. We illustrate the intuition behind EPHAD using a synthetic toy example and validate its effectiveness through comprehensive experiments across eight visual AD datasets, twenty-six tabular AD datasets, and a real-world industrial AD dataset. Additionally, we conduct an ablation study to analyse hyperparameter influence and robustness to varying contamination levels, demonstrating the versatility and robustness of EPHAD across diverse AD models and evidence pairs. To ensure reproducibility, our code is publicly available at https://github.com/sukanyapatra1997/EPHAD."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted in the Thirty-ninth Annual Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2025)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21296","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:51:23Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:51:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-30T03:42:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20984","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20984","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20984","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaolin","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xiaolin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiamang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jiamang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weisi","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Weisi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning Grouped Lattice Vector Quantizers for Low-Bit LLM Compression"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T20:19:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:07:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-26T12:51:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-27T02:26:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable capabilities but typically require extensive computational resources and memory for inference. Post-training quantization (PTQ) can effectively reduce these demands by storing weights in lower bit-width formats. However, standard uniform quantization often leads to notable performance degradation, particularly in low-bit scenarios. In this work, we introduce a Grouped Lattice Vector Quantization (GLVQ) framework that assigns each group of weights a customized lattice codebook, defined by a learnable generation matrix. To address the non-differentiability of the quantization process, we adopt Babai rounding to approximate nearest-lattice-point search during training, which enables stable optimization of the generation matrices. Once trained, decoding reduces to a simple matrix-vector multiplication, yielding an efficient and practical quantization pipeline. Experiments on multiple benchmarks show that our approach achieves a better trade-off between model size and accuracy compared to existing post-training quantization baselines, highlighting its effectiveness in deploying large models under stringent resource constraints. Our source code is available on GitHub repository: https://github.com/xzhang9308/GLVQ."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2025 Poster"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20984","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:44:03Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:44:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-27T04:37:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21352","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21352","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21352","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mariam","familyName":"Arustashvili","name":"Arustashvili, Mariam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Krisztian","familyName":"Balog","name":"Balog, Krisztian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SciNUP: Natural Language User Interest Profiles for Scientific Literature Recommendation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T11:28:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:43:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-23T12:48:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-26T01:35:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The use of natural language (NL) user profiles in recommender systems offers greater transparency and user control compared to traditional representations. However, there is scarcity of large-scale, publicly available test collections for evaluating NL profile-based recommendation. To address this gap, we introduce SciNUP, a novel synthetic dataset for scholarly recommendation that leverages authors' publication histories to generate NL profiles and corresponding ground truth items. We use this dataset to conduct a comparison of baseline methods, ranging from sparse and dense retrieval approaches to state-of-the-art LLM-based rerankers. Our results show that while baseline methods achieve comparable performance, they often retrieve different items, indicating complementary behaviors. At the same time, considerable headroom for improvement remains, highlighting the need for effective NL-based recommendation approaches. The SciNUP dataset thus serves as a valuable resource for fostering future research and development in this area."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Proceedings of the 48th European Conference on Information Retrieval (ECIR '26), 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21352","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:52:42Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:52:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-26T02:50:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21310","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21310","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21310","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ji Won","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Ji Won","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyunghyun","familyName":"Cho","name":"Cho, Kyunghyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Efficient semantic uncertainty quantification in language models via diversity-steered sampling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T10:06:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:39:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-23T18:02:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-26T01:49:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Accurately estimating semantic aleatoric and epistemic uncertainties in large language models (LLMs) is particularly challenging in free-form question answering (QA), where obtaining stable estimates often requires many expensive generations. We introduce a diversity-steered sampler that discourages semantically redundant outputs during decoding, covers both autoregressive and masked diffusion paradigms, and yields substantial sample-efficiency gains. The key idea is to inject a continuous semantic-similarity penalty into the model's proposal distribution using a natural language inference (NLI) model lightly finetuned on partial prefixes or intermediate diffusion states. We debias downstream uncertainty estimates with importance reweighting and shrink their variance with control variates. Across four QA benchmarks, our method matches or surpasses baselines while covering more semantic clusters with the same number of samples. Being modular and requiring no gradient access to the base LLM, the framework promises to serve as a drop-in enhancement for uncertainty estimation in risk-sensitive model deployments."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages (+7 appendix), 7 figures. Accepted at NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21310","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:51:43Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:51:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-26T02:50:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21182","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21182","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21182","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junzhe","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Junzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huixuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Huixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaojun","familyName":"Wan","name":"Wan, Xiaojun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"KBE-DME: Dynamic Multimodal Evaluation via Knowledge Enhanced Benchmark Evolution"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T06:13:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:27:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-21T07:52:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-22T01:28:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid progress of multimodal large language models (MLLMs) calls for more reliable evaluation protocols. Existing static benchmarks suffer from the potential risk of data contamination and saturation, leading to inflated or misleading performance evaluations. To address these issues, we first apply Graph formulation to represent a static or dynamic VQA sample. With the formulation, we propose Knowledge-enhanced Benchmark Evolution(KBE), a dynamic multimodal evaluation framework. KBE first analyzes the original static benchmark, then expands it by integrating multimodal knowledge, transforming the static benchmark into a controllable, dynamic evolving version. Crucially, KBE can both reconstruct questions by Re-selecting visual information in the original image and expand existing questions with external textual knowledge. It enables difficulty-controllable evaluation by adjusting the degree of question exploration. Extensive experiments demonstrate that KBE alleviates the risk of data contamination, data saturation, and provides a more comprehensive assessment of MLLM capabilities."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21182","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:48:40Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:48:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-22T03:18:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21057","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21057","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21057","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nils Philipp","familyName":"Walter","name":"Walter, Nils Philipp","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chawin","familyName":"Sitawarin","name":"Sitawarin, Chawin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jamie","familyName":"Hayes","name":"Hayes, Jamie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Stutz","name":"Stutz, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ilia","familyName":"Shumailov","name":"Shumailov, Ilia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Soft Instruction De-escalation Defense"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T00:04:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:13:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-17T09:50:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-21T01:24:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed in agentic systems that interact with an external environment; this makes them susceptible to prompt injections when dealing with untrusted data. To overcome this limitation, we propose SIC (Soft Instruction Control)-a simple yet effective iterative prompt sanitization loop designed for tool-augmented LLM agents. Our method repeatedly inspects incoming data for instructions that could compromise agent behavior. If such content is found, the malicious content is rewritten, masked, or removed, and the result is re-evaluated. The process continues until the input is clean or a maximum iteration limit is reached; if imperative instruction-like content remains, the agent halts to ensure security. By allowing multiple passes, our approach acknowledges that individual rewrites may fail but enables the system to catch and correct missed injections in later steps. Although immediately useful, worst-case analysis shows that SIC is not infallible; strong adversary can still get a 15% ASR by embedding non-imperative workflows. This nonetheless raises the bar."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21057","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:45:44Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:45:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-21T06:07:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20941","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20941","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20941","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaromir","familyName":"Savelka","name":"Savelka, Jaromir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Ashley","name":"Ashley, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Do LLMs Truly Understand When a Precedent Is Overruled?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; I.2.4","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T50","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T19:07:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-21T01:58:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.3233/faia251592","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) with extended context windows show promise for complex legal reasoning tasks, yet their ability to understand long legal documents remains insufficiently evaluated. Developing long-context benchmarks that capture realistic, high-stakes tasks remains a significant challenge in the field, as most existing evaluations rely on simplified synthetic tasks that fail to represent the complexity of real-world document understanding. Overruling relationships are foundational to common-law doctrine and commonly found in judicial opinions. They provide a focused and important testbed for long-document legal understanding that closely resembles what legal professionals actually do. We present an assessment of state-of-the-art LLMs on identifying overruling relationships from U.S. Supreme Court cases using a dataset of 236 case pairs. Our evaluation reveals three critical limitations: (1) era sensitivity -- the models show degraded performance on historical cases compared to modern ones, revealing fundamental temporal bias in their training; (2) shallow reasoning -- models rely on shallow logical heuristics rather than deep legal comprehension; and (3) context-dependent reasoning failures -- models produce temporally impossible relationships in complex open-ended tasks despite maintaining basic temporal awareness in simple contexts. Our work contributes a benchmark that addresses the critical gap in realistic long-context evaluation, providing an environment that mirrors the complexity and stakes of actual legal reasoning tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 2 figures, JURIX 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20941","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-27T01:43:03Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:43:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-21T06:07:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20926","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.20926","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.20926","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Natasha","familyName":"Johnson","name":"Johnson, Natasha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amanda","familyName":"Bertsch","name":"Bertsch, Amanda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maria-Emil","familyName":"Deal","name":"Deal, Maria-Emil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emma","familyName":"Strubell","name":"Strubell, Emma","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FicSim: A Dataset for Multi-Faceted Semantic Similarity in Long-Form Fiction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T18:30:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:03:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-19T19:32:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-21T02:42:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.18653/v1/2025.findings-emnlp.1375","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As language models become capable of processing increasingly long and complex texts, there has been growing interest in their application within computational literary studies. However, evaluating the usefulness of these models for such tasks remains challenging due to the cost of fine-grained annotation for long-form texts and the data contamination concerns inherent in using public-domain literature. Current embedding similarity datasets are not suitable for evaluating literary-domain tasks because of a focus on coarse-grained similarity and primarily on very short text. We assemble and release FICSIM, a dataset of long-form, recently written fiction, including scores along 12 axes of similarity informed by author-produced metadata and validated by digital humanities scholars. We evaluate a suite of embedding models on this task, demonstrating a tendency across models to focus on surface-level features over semantic categories that would be useful for computational literary studies tasks. Throughout our data-collection process, we prioritize author agency and rely on continual, informed author consent."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published in Findings of EMNLP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.20926","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-27T01:42:41Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:42:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-21T06:07:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21518","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21518","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21518","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lorenzo","familyName":"Basile","name":"Basile, Lorenzo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Valentino","familyName":"Maiorca","name":"Maiorca, Valentino","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Diego","familyName":"Doimo","name":"Doimo, Diego","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francesco","familyName":"Locatello","name":"Locatello, Francesco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alberto","familyName":"Cazzaniga","name":"Cazzaniga, Alberto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Head Pursuit: Probing Attention Specialization in Multimodal Transformers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T14:41:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:54:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-14T15:47:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-15T01:48:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Language and vision-language models have shown impressive performance across a wide range of tasks, but their internal mechanisms remain only partly understood. In this work, we study how individual attention heads in text-generative models specialize in specific semantic or visual attributes. Building on an established interpretability method, we reinterpret the practice of probing intermediate activations with the final decoding layer through the lens of signal processing. This lets us analyze multiple samples in a principled way and rank attention heads based on their relevance to target concepts. Our results show consistent patterns of specialization at the head level across both unimodal and multimodal transformers. Remarkably, we find that editing as few as 1% of the heads, selected using our method, can reliably suppress or enhance targeted concepts in the model output. We validate our approach on language tasks such as question answering and toxicity mitigation, as well as vision-language tasks including image classification and captioning. Our findings highlight an interpretable and controllable structure within attention layers, offering simple tools for understanding and editing large-scale generative models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at NeurIPS 2025 (spotlight)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21518","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:56:33Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:56:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-15T03:11:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21144","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21144","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21144","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanyu","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Hanyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lance","familyName":"Fiondella","name":"Fiondella, Lance","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawei","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Jiawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Long","familyName":"Jiao","name":"Jiao, Long","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"NeuroGenPoisoning: Neuron-Guided Attacks on Retrieval-Augmented Generation of LLM via Genetic Optimization of External Knowledge"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T04:30:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-04T02:06:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-11T21:36:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-13T01:56:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) empowers Large Language Models (LLMs) to dynamically integrate external knowledge during inference, improving their factual accuracy and adaptability. However, adversaries can inject poisoned external knowledge to override the model's internal memory. While existing attacks iteratively manipulate retrieval content or prompt structure of RAG, they largely ignore the model's internal representation dynamics and neuron-level sensitivities. The underlying mechanism of RAG poisoning has not been fully studied and the effect of knowledge conflict with strong parametric knowledge in RAG is not considered. In this work, we propose NeuroGenPoisoning, a novel attack framework that generates adversarial external knowledge in RAG guided by LLM internal neuron attribution and genetic optimization. Our method first identifies a set of Poison-Responsive Neurons whose activation strongly correlates with contextual poisoning knowledge. We then employ a genetic algorithm to evolve adversarial passages that maximally activate these neurons. Crucially, our framework enables massive-scale generation of effective poisoned RAG knowledge by identifying and reusing promising but initially unsuccessful external knowledge variants via observed attribution signals. At the same time, Poison-Responsive Neurons guided poisoning can effectively resolves knowledge conflict. Experimental results across models and datasets demonstrate consistently achieving high Population Overwrite Success Rate (POSR) of over 90% while preserving fluency. Empirical evidence shows that our method effectively resolves knowledge conflict."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21144","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:47:47Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:47:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-13T04:27:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21007","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21007","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21007","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samuel","familyName":"Lewis-Lim","name":"Lewis-Lim, Samuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingwei","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Xingwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhixue","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Zhixue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikolaos","familyName":"Aletras","name":"Aletras, Nikolaos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Can Confidence Estimates Decide When Chain-of-Thought Is Necessary for LLMs?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T21:33:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:09:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T09:25:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:32:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-08T16:05:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-09T01:50:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Chain-of-thought (CoT) prompting is a common technique for improving the reasoning abilities of large language models (LLMs). However, extended reasoning is often unnecessary and substantially increases token usage. As such, a key question becomes how to optimally allocate compute to when reasoning is actually needed. We study this through confidence-gated CoT, where a model produces a direct answer and a confidence estimate to decide whether to invoke CoT. We present an evaluation framework together with the first systematic study of confidence signals for this decision. We evaluate four representative confidence measures and compare them with random gating and an oracle upper bound. Experiments across two model families and diverse reasoning tasks show that existing training-free confidence measures can reduce redundant reasoning. However, we also find that the utility of individual confidence measures is inconsistent across settings. Through our evaluation framework and analysis, our study provides practical guidance toward developing and evaluating models that selectively use CoT."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Under Review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21007","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:44:35Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:44:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-09T03:11:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21118","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21118","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21118","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiang","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Qiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lvzhou","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Lvzhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixuan","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Yixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ping","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Ping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Gray Zone of Faithfulness: Taming Ambiguity in Unfaithfulness Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T03:13:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:21:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T02:50:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:18:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-15T02:10:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-16T02:08:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-29T02:55:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-12-30T01:51:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Ensuring that Large Language Models (LLMs) generate summaries faithful to a given source document is essential for real-world applications. While prior research has explored LLM faithfulness, existing benchmarks suffer from annotation ambiguity, primarily due to the ill-defined boundary of permissible external knowledge in generated outputs. For instance, common sense is often incorporated into responses and labeled as \"faithful\", yet the acceptable extent of such knowledge remains unspecified, leading to inconsistent annotations. To address this issue, we propose a novel faithfulness annotation framework, which introduces an intermediate category, Out-Dependent, to classify cases where external knowledge is required for verification. Using this framework, we construct VeriGray (Verification with the Gray Zone) -- a new unfaithfulness detection benchmark in summarization. Statistics reveal that even SOTA LLMs, such as GPT-5, exhibit hallucinations ($\\sim 6\\%$ of sentences) in summarization tasks. Moreover, a substantial proportion ($\\sim 9\\%$ on average of models) of generated sentences fall into the Out-Dependent category, underscoring the importance of resolving annotation ambiguity in unfaithfulness detection benchmarks. Experiments demonstrate that our benchmark poses significant challenges to multiple baseline methods, indicating considerable room for future improvement."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Update the evaluation results due to the annotation updates; revise the citation to Seo et al., 2025; add the acknowledgements"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21118","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:47:10Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:47:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-30T03:52:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21424","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21424","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21424","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abderrazek","familyName":"Abid","name":"Abid, Abderrazek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thanh-Cong","familyName":"Ho","name":"Ho, Thanh-Cong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fakhri","familyName":"Karray","name":"Karray, Fakhri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Vision Language Models for Dynamic Human Activity Recognition in Healthcare Settings"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T13:04:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-18T02:48:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1007/978-3-032-08452-1_4","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As generative AI continues to evolve, Vision Language Models (VLMs) have emerged as promising tools in various healthcare applications. One area that remains relatively underexplored is their use in human activity recognition (HAR) for remote health monitoring. VLMs offer notable strengths, including greater flexibility and the ability to overcome some of the constraints of traditional deep learning models. However, a key challenge in applying VLMs to HAR lies in the difficulty of evaluating their dynamic and often non-deterministic outputs. To address this gap, we introduce a descriptive caption data set and propose comprehensive evaluation methods to evaluate VLMs in HAR. Through comparative experiments with state-of-the-art deep learning models, our findings demonstrate that VLMs achieve comparable performance and, in some cases, even surpass conventional approaches in terms of accuracy. This work contributes a strong benchmark and opens new possibilities for the integration of VLMs into intelligent healthcare systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21424","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-27T01:54:23Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:54:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-18T05:17:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21341","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21341","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21341","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lufan","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Lufan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Magellan: Guided MCTS for Latent Space Exploration and Novelty Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T11:09:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:42:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-17T02:33:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-18T02:19:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) often struggle with generating truly innovative ideas, typically defaulting to high-probability, familiar concepts within their training data's \"gravity wells.\" While advanced search-based methods like Tree of Thoughts (ToT) attempt to mitigate this, they are fundamentally limited by their reliance on unprincipled, inconsistent self-evaluation heuristics to guide exploration. To address this gap, we introduce \\textbf{Magellan}, a novel framework that reframes creative generation as a principled, guided exploration of an LLM's latent conceptual space. At its core, Magellan employs Monte Carlo Tree Search (MCTS) governed by a hierarchical guidance system. For long-range direction, a \"semantic compass\" vector, formulated via orthogonal projection, steers the search towards relevant novelty. For local, step-by-step decisions, a landscape-aware value function replaces flawed self-evaluation with an explicit reward structure that balances intrinsic coherence, extrinsic novelty, and narrative progress. Extensive experiments demonstrate that Magellan significantly outperforms strong baselines, including ReAct and ToT, in generating scientific ideas with superior plausibility and innovation. Our work shows that for creative discovery, a principled, guided search is more effective than unconstrained agency, paving the way for LLMs to become more capable partners in innovation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to 1st Open Conference on AI Agents for Science (agents4science 2025)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21341","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:52:27Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:52:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-18T05:17:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21244","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21244","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21244","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengyu","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Pengyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shijia","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Shijia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ao","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Ao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Feng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yahan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yahan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhanyu","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Zhanyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiguo","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jiguo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiuchong","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Jiuchong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinghua","familyName":"Hao","name":"Hao, Jinghua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Renqing","familyName":"He","name":"He, Renqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaobo","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Xiaobo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fan","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Fan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jia","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Jia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanghua","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Guanghua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VoiceAgentEval: A Dual-Dimensional Benchmark for Expert-Level Intelligent Voice-Agent Evaluation of Xbench's Professional-Aligned Series"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T08:27:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:33:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-14T11:59:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-17T01:38:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose OutboundEval, a comprehensive benchmark for evaluating large language models (LLMs) in expert-level intelligent outbound calling scenarios. Unlike existing methods that suffer from three key limitations - insufficient dataset diversity and category coverage, unrealistic user simulation, and inaccurate evaluation metrics - OutboundEval addresses these issues through a structured framework. First, we design a benchmark spanning six major business domains and 30 representative sub-scenarios, each with scenario-specific process decomposition, weighted scoring, and domain-adaptive metrics. Second, we develop a large-model-driven User Simulator that generates diverse, persona-rich virtual users with realistic behaviors, emotional variability, and communication styles, providing a controlled yet authentic testing environment. Third, we introduce a dynamic evaluation method that adapts to task variations, integrating automated and human-in-the-loop assessment to measure task execution accuracy, professional knowledge application, adaptability, and user experience quality. Experiments on 12 state-of-the-art LLMs reveal distinct trade-offs between expert-level task completion and interaction fluency, offering practical insights for building reliable, human-like outbound AI systems. OutboundEval establishes a practical, extensible, and domain-oriented standard for benchmarking LLMs in professional applications."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21244","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:50:10Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:50:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-17T03:06:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21412","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21412","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21412","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Whie","familyName":"Jung","name":"Jung, Whie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Semin","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Semin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junee","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Junee","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seunghoon","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Seunghoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Bridging the gap to real-world language-grounded visual concept learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T12:54:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:47:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T05:32:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-11T02:19:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Human intelligence effortlessly interprets visual scenes along a rich spectrum of semantic dimensions. However, existing approaches to language-grounded visual concept learning are limited to a few predefined primitive axes, such as color and shape, and are typically explored in synthetic datasets. In this work, we propose a scalable framework that adaptively identifies image-related concept axes and grounds visual concepts along these axes in real-world scenes. Leveraging a pretrained vision-language model and our universal prompting strategy, our framework identifies a diverse image-related axes without any prior knowledge. Our universal concept encoder adaptively binds visual features to the discovered axes without introducing additional model parameters for each concept. To ground visual concepts along the discovered axes, we optimize a compositional anchoring objective, which ensures that each axis can be independently manipulated without affecting others. We demonstrate the effectiveness of our framework on subsets of ImageNet, CelebA-HQ, and AFHQ, showcasing superior editing capabilities across diverse real-world concepts that are too varied to be manually predefined. Our method also exhibits strong compositional generalization, outperforming existing visual concept learning and text-based editing methods. The code is available at https://github.com/whieya/Language-grounded-VCL."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21412","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:54:07Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:54:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-12T03:31:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21450","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21450","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21450","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Federico","familyName":"Danieli","name":"Danieli, Federico","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pau","familyName":"Rodriguez","name":"Rodriguez, Pau","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miguel","familyName":"Sarabia","name":"Sarabia, Miguel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xavier","familyName":"Suau","name":"Suau, Xavier","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luca","familyName":"Zappella","name":"Zappella, Luca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ParaRNN: Unlocking Parallel Training of Nonlinear RNNs for Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T07, 68W10","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T13:28:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:50:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-03T09:47:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-04T02:26:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs) laid the foundation for sequence modeling, but their intrinsic sequential nature restricts parallel computation, creating a fundamental barrier to scaling. This has led to the dominance of parallelizable architectures like Transformers and, more recently, State Space Models (SSMs). While SSMs achieve efficient parallelization through structured linear recurrences, this linearity constraint limits their expressive power and precludes modeling complex, nonlinear sequence-wise dependencies. To address this, we present ParaRNN, a framework that breaks the sequence-parallelization barrier for nonlinear RNNs. Building on prior work, we cast the sequence of nonlinear recurrence relationships as a single system of equations, which we solve in parallel using Newton's iterations combined with custom parallel reductions. Our implementation achieves speedups of up to 665x over naive sequential application, allowing training nonlinear RNNs at unprecedented scales. To showcase this, we apply ParaRNN to adaptations of LSTM and GRU architectures, successfully training models of 7B parameters that attain perplexity comparable to similarly-sized Transformers and Mamba2 architectures. To accelerate research in efficient sequence modeling, we release the ParaRNN codebase as an open-source framework for automatic training-parallelization of nonlinear RNNs, enabling researchers and practitioners to explore new nonlinear RNN models at scale."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21450","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:55:00Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:55:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-04T04:59:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21513","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21513","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21513","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fernando","familyName":"Vallecillos-Ruiz","name":"Vallecillos-Ruiz, Fernando","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Max","familyName":"Hort","name":"Hort, Max","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leon","familyName":"Moonen","name":"Moonen, Leon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Wisdom and Delusion of LLM Ensembles for Code Generation and Repair"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T14:39:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:54:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-30T13:03:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-31T00:45:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Today's pursuit of a single Large Language Model (LMM) for all software engineering tasks is resource-intensive and overlooks the potential benefits of complementarity, where different models contribute unique strengths. However, the degree to which coding LLMs complement each other and the best strategy for maximizing an ensemble's potential are unclear, leaving practitioners without a clear path to move beyond single-model systems. To address this gap, we empirically compare ten individual LLMs from five families, and three ensembles of these LLMs across three software engineering benchmarks covering code generation and program repair. We assess the complementarity between models and the performance gap between the best individual model and the ensembles. Next, we evaluate various selection heuristics to identify correct solutions from an ensemble's candidate pool. We find that the theoretical upperbound for an ensemble's performance can be 83% above the best single model. Our results show that consensus-based strategies for selecting solutions fall into a \"popularity trap,\" amplifying common but incorrect outputs. In contrast, a diversity-based strategy realizes up to 95% of this theoretical potential, and proves effective even in small two-model ensembles, enabling a cost-efficient way to enhance performance by leveraging multiple LLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Added Acknowledgments section and hyphenated last names"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21513","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:56:26Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:56:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-31T02:11:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21614","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21614","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21614","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenyi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wenyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Piotr","familyName":"Piękos","name":"Piękos, Piotr","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Nanbo","name":"Nanbo, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Firas","familyName":"Laakom","name":"Laakom, Firas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yimeng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yimeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mateusz","familyName":"Ostaszewski","name":"Ostaszewski, Mateusz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingchen","familyName":"Zhuge","name":"Zhuge, Mingchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jürgen","familyName":"Schmidhuber","name":"Schmidhuber, Jürgen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Huxley-Gödel Machine: Human-Level Coding Agent Development by an Approximation of the Optimal Self-Improving Machine"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T16:19:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T01:00:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T14:03:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-29T00:53:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-29T13:57:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-30T00:50:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent studies operationalize self-improvement through coding agents that edit their own codebases. They grow a tree of self-modifications through expansion strategies that favor higher software engineering benchmark performance, assuming that this implies more promising subsequent self-modifications. However, we identify a mismatch between the agent's self-improvement potential (metaproductivity) and its coding benchmark performance, namely the Metaproductivity-Performance Mismatch. Inspired by Huxley's concept of clade, we propose a metric ($\\mathrm{CMP}$) that aggregates the benchmark performances of the descendants of an agent as an indicator of its potential for self-improvement. We show that, in our self-improving coding agent development setting, access to the true $\\mathrm{CMP}$ is sufficient to simulate how the Gödel Machine would behave under certain assumptions. We introduce the Huxley-Gödel Machine (HGM), which, by estimating $\\mathrm{CMP}$ and using it as guidance, searches the tree of self-modifications. On SWE-bench Verified and Polyglot, HGM outperforms prior self-improving coding agent development methods while using fewer allocated CPU hours. Last but not least, HGM demonstrates strong transfer to other coding datasets and large language models. The agent optimized by HGM on SWE-bench Verified with GPT-5-mini and evaluated on SWE-bench Lite with GPT-5 achieves human-level performance, matching the best officially checked results of human-engineered coding agents. Our code is publicly available at https://github.com/metauto-ai/HGM."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21614","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:58:44Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:58:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-30T02:19:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21566","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21566","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21566","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fangwen","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Fangwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jihong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jihong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunku","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yunku","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruiguang","familyName":"Pei","name":"Pei, Ruiguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heyuan","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Heyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingyu","familyName":"Lou","name":"Lou, Xingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huarong","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Huarong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhihui","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Zhihui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiwen","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Weiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuosheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhuosheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weinan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Weinan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ColorEcosystem: Powering Personalized, Standardized, and Trustworthy Agentic Service in massive-agent Ecosystem"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T15:26:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:57:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T07:12:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:26:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the rapid development of (multimodal) large language model-based agents, the landscape of agentic service management has evolved from single-agent systems to multi-agent systems, and now to massive-agent ecosystems. Current massive-agent ecosystems face growing challenges, including impersonal service experiences, a lack of standardization, and untrustworthy behavior. To address these issues, we propose ColorEcosystem, a novel blueprint designed to enable personalized, standardized, and trustworthy agentic service at scale. Concretely, ColorEcosystem consists of three key components: agent carrier, agent store, and agent audit. The agent carrier provides personalized service experiences by utilizing user-specific data and creating a digital twin, while the agent store serves as a centralized, standardized platform for managing diverse agentic services. The agent audit, based on the supervision of developer and user activities, ensures the integrity and credibility of both service providers and users. Through the analysis of challenges, transitional forms, and practical considerations, the ColorEcosystem is poised to power personalized, standardized, and trustworthy agentic service across massive-agent ecosystems. Meanwhile, we have also implemented part of ColorEcosystem's functionality, and the relevant code is open-sourced at https://github.com/opas-lab/color-ecosystem."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21566","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:57:39Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:57:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T04:06:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21339","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21339","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21339","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Qiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wuganjing","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Wuganjing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenzhou","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Zhenzhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feifan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Feifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiaolong","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Qiaolong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongduo","familyName":"Sui","name":"Sui, Yongduo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-turn Training with Basic Human Feedback Helps Little on LLM Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T11:08:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:42:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T11:23:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:37:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The reasoning capabilities of Large Language Models (LLMs) are typically developed through the single-turn reinforcement learning, whereas real-world applications often involve multi-turn interactions with human feedback, leading to a potential mismatch between training and deployment conditions. In this work, we study whether multi-turn training with human feedback is necessary for reasoning tasks. We compare conventional single-turn training with three multi-turn strategies and reach contrary conclusions to previous research. We find that models trained in a single-turn setting generalize effectively to both single- and multi-turn evaluations, while models trained with multi-turn strategies exhibit a significant degradation in single-turn reasoning performance. These results suggest that for tasks with complete information, robust single-turn training remains more effective and reliable, as multi-turn training with basic feedback provides limited benefits and can even degrade reasoning capabilities."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21339","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:52:24Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:52:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T04:06:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21059","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21059","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21059","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mahmud Wasif","familyName":"Nafee","name":"Nafee, Mahmud Wasif","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maiqi","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Maiqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haipeng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Haipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanfu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yanfu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Dynamic Retriever for In-Context Knowledge Editing via Policy Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T00:15:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:13:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:25:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:13:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) excel at factual recall yet still propagate stale or incorrect knowledge. In-context knowledge editing offers a gradient-free remedy suitable for black-box APIs, but current editors rely on static demonstration sets chosen by surface-level similarity, leading to two persistent obstacles: (i) a quantity-quality trade-off, and (ii) lack of adaptivity to task difficulty. We address these issues by dynamically selecting supporting demonstrations according to their utility for the edit. We propose Dynamic Retriever for In-Context Knowledge Editing (DR-IKE), a lightweight framework that (1) trains a BERT retriever with REINFORCE to rank demonstrations by editing reward, and (2) employs a learnable threshold to prune low-value examples, shortening the prompt when the edit is easy and expanding it when the task is hard. DR-IKE performs editing without modifying model weights, relying solely on forward passes for compatibility with black-box LLMs. On the COUNTERFACT benchmark, it improves edit success by up to 17.1%, reduces latency by 41.6%, and preserves accuracy on unrelated queries, demonstrating scalable and adaptive knowledge editing. The code is available at https://github.com/mwnafee/DR-IKE ."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at EMNLP 2025. Copyright 2025 Association for Computational Linguistics (CC BY 4.0). 12 pages, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21059","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:45:46Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:45:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T04:06:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21034","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21034","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21034","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Barkavi","familyName":"Sundararajan","name":"Sundararajan, Barkavi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Somayajulu","familyName":"Sripada","name":"Sripada, Somayajulu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ehud","familyName":"Reiter","name":"Reiter, Ehud","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Input Matters: Evaluating Input Structure's Impact on LLM Summaries of Sports Play-by-Play"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T22:36:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:11:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T11:49:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:38:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A major concern when deploying LLMs in accuracy-critical domains such as sports reporting is that the generated text may not faithfully reflect the input data. We quantify how input structure affects hallucinations and other factual errors in LLM-generated summaries of NBA play-by-play data, across three formats: row-structured, JSON and unstructured. We manually annotated 3,312 factual errors across 180 game summaries produced by two models, Llama-3.1-70B and Qwen2.5-72B. Input structure has a strong effect: JSON input reduces error rates by 69% for Llama and 65% for Qwen compared to unstructured input, while row-structured input reduces errors by 54% for Llama and 51% for Qwen. A two-way repeated measures ANOVA shows that input structure accounts for over 80% of the variance in error rates, with Tukey HSD post hoc tests confirming statistically significant differences between all input formats."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at INLG 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21034","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:45:12Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:45:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T04:06:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21684","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21684","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21684","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Albert","familyName":"Cheu","name":"Cheu, Albert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Artem","familyName":"Lagzdin","name":"Lagzdin, Artem","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brett","familyName":"McLarnon","name":"McLarnon, Brett","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Ramage","name":"Ramage, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Katharine","familyName":"Daly","name":"Daly, Katharine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Gruteser","name":"Gruteser, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Kairouz","name":"Kairouz, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rakshita","familyName":"Tandon","name":"Tandon, Rakshita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stanislav","familyName":"Chiknavaryan","name":"Chiknavaryan, Stanislav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Timon","familyName":"Van Overveldt","name":"Van Overveldt, Timon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zoe","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Zoe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Toward provably private analytics and insights into GenAI use"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T17:40:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T01:04:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large-scale systems that compute analytics over a fleet of devices must achieve high privacy and security standards while also meeting data quality, usability, and resource efficiency expectations. We present a next-generation federated analytics system that uses Trusted Execution Environments (TEEs) based on technologies like AMD SEV-SNP and Intel TDX to provide verifiable privacy guarantees for all server-side processing. In our system, devices encrypt and upload data, tagging it with a limited set of allowable server-side processing steps. An open source, TEE-hosted key management service guarantees that the data is accessible only to those steps, which are themselves protected by TEE confidentiality and integrity assurance guarantees. The system is designed for flexible workloads, including processing unstructured data with LLMs (for structured summarization) before aggregation into differentially private insights (with automatic parameter tuning). The transparency properties of our system allow any external party to verify that all raw and derived data is processed in TEEs, protecting it from inspection by the system operator, and that differential privacy is applied to all released results. This system has been successfully deployed in production, providing helpful insights into real-world GenAI experiences."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21684","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T02:00:22Z","registered":"2025-10-27T02:00:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T02:00:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21682","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21682","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21682","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sikuang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Sikuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiemin","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Jiemin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taoran","familyName":"Yi","name":"Yi, Taoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jia","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Jia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiazhong","familyName":"Cen","name":"Cen, Jiazhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingxi","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Lingxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"WorldGrow: Generating Infinite 3D World"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Graphics (cs.GR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T17:39:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T01:04:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We tackle the challenge of generating the infinitely extendable 3D world -- large, continuous environments with coherent geometry and realistic appearance. Existing methods face key challenges: 2D-lifting approaches suffer from geometric and appearance inconsistencies across views, 3D implicit representations are hard to scale up, and current 3D foundation models are mostly object-centric, limiting their applicability to scene-level generation. Our key insight is leveraging strong generation priors from pre-trained 3D models for structured scene block generation. To this end, we propose WorldGrow, a hierarchical framework for unbounded 3D scene synthesis. Our method features three core components: (1) a data curation pipeline that extracts high-quality scene blocks for training, making the 3D structured latent representations suitable for scene generation; (2) a 3D block inpainting mechanism that enables context-aware scene extension; and (3) a coarse-to-fine generation strategy that ensures both global layout plausibility and local geometric/textural fidelity. Evaluated on the large-scale 3D-FRONT dataset, WorldGrow achieves SOTA performance in geometry reconstruction, while uniquely supporting infinite scene generation with photorealistic and structurally consistent outputs. These results highlight its capability for constructing large-scale virtual environments and potential for building future world models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project page: https://world-grow.github.io/ Code: https://github.com/world-grow/WorldGrow"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21682","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T02:00:19Z","registered":"2025-10-27T02:00:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T02:00:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21679","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21679","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21679","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gaku","familyName":"Morio","name":"Morio, Gaku","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harri","familyName":"Rowlands","name":"Rowlands, Harri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dominik","familyName":"Stammbach","name":"Stammbach, Dominik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christopher D.","familyName":"Manning","name":"Manning, Christopher D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Henderson","name":"Henderson, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Multimodal Benchmark for Framing of Oil \u0026amp; Gas Advertising and Potential Greenwashing Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T17:34:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T01:03:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Companies spend large amounts of money on public relations campaigns to project a positive brand image. However, sometimes there is a mismatch between what they say and what they do. Oil \u0026amp; gas companies, for example, are accused of \"greenwashing\" with imagery of climate-friendly initiatives. Understanding the framing, and changes in framing, at scale can help better understand the goals and nature of public relations campaigns. To address this, we introduce a benchmark dataset of expert-annotated video ads obtained from Facebook and YouTube. The dataset provides annotations for 13 framing types for more than 50 companies or advocacy groups across 20 countries. Our dataset is especially designed for the evaluation of vision-language models (VLMs), distinguishing it from past text-only framing datasets. Baseline experiments show some promising results, while leaving room for improvement for future work: GPT-4.1 can detect environmental messages with 79% F1 score, while our best model only achieves 46% F1 score on identifying framing around green innovation. We also identify challenges that VLMs must address, such as implicit framing, handling videos of various lengths, or implicit cultural backgrounds. Our dataset contributes to research in multimodal analysis of strategic communication in the energy sector."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Forthcoming in NeurIPS 2025 Datasets and Benchmarks Track"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21679","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T02:00:14Z","registered":"2025-10-27T02:00:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T02:00:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21671","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21671","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21671","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yabo","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Yabo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Xi","name":"Xi, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jialong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jialong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shanqi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shanqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiateng","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Jiateng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Data-Centric Approach to Multilingual E-Commerce Product Search: Case Study on Query-Category and Query-Item Relevance"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T17:27:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T01:03:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multilingual e-commerce search suffers from severe data imbalance across languages, label noise, and limited supervision for low-resource languages--challenges that impede the cross-lingual generalization of relevance models despite the strong capabilities of large language models (LLMs). In this work, we present a practical, architecture-agnostic, data-centric framework to enhance performance on two core tasks: Query-Category (QC) relevance (matching queries to product categories) and Query-Item (QI) relevance (matching queries to product titles). Rather than altering the model, we redesign the training data through three complementary strategies: (1) translation-based augmentation to synthesize examples for languages absent in training, (2) semantic negative sampling to generate hard negatives and mitigate class imbalance, and (3) self-validation filtering to detect and remove likely mislabeled instances. Evaluated on the CIKM AnalytiCup 2025 dataset, our approach consistently yields substantial F1 score improvements over strong LLM baselines, achieving competitive results in the official competition. Our findings demonstrate that systematic data engineering can be as impactful as--and often more deployable than--complex model modifications, offering actionable guidance for building robust multilingual search systems in the real-world e-commerce settings."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21671","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T02:00:03Z","registered":"2025-10-27T02:00:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T02:00:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21664","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21664","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21664","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Riya","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Riya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiwei","familyName":"Zong","name":"Zong, Yiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dennis H.","familyName":"Murphree","name":"Murphree, Dennis H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Foundation Models in Dermatopathology: Skin Tissue Classification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantitative Methods (q-bio.QM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T17:21:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T01:03:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid generation of whole-slide images (WSIs) in dermatopathology necessitates automated methods for efficient processing and accurate classification. This study evaluates the performance of two foundation models, UNI and Virchow2, as feature extractors for classifying WSIs into three diagnostic categories: melanocytic, basaloid, and squamous lesions. Patch-level embeddings were aggregated into slide-level features using a mean-aggregation strategy and subsequently used to train multiple machine learning classifiers, including logistic regression, gradient-boosted trees, and random forest models. Performance was assessed using precision, recall, true positive rate, false positive rate, and the area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUROC) on the test set. Results demonstrate that patch-level features extracted using Virchow2 outperformed those extracted via UNI across most slide-level classifiers, with logistic regression achieving the highest accuracy (90%) for Virchow2, though the difference was not statistically significant. The study also explored data augmentation techniques and image normalization to enhance model robustness and generalizability. The mean-aggregation approach provided reliable slide-level feature representations. All experimental results and metrics were tracked and visualized using WandB.ai, facilitating reproducibility and interpretability. This research highlights the potential of foundation models for automated WSI classification, providing a scalable and effective approach for dermatopathological diagnosis while paving the way for future advancements in slide-level representation learning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21664","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:59:53Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:59:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:59:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21631","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21631","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21631","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Faisal","familyName":"Hamman","name":"Hamman, Faisal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pasan","familyName":"Dissanayake","name":"Dissanayake, Pasan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanjun","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Yanjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanghamitra","familyName":"Dutta","name":"Dutta, Sanghamitra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Few-Shot Knowledge Distillation of LLMs With Counterfactual Explanations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T16:36:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T01:01:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Knowledge distillation is a promising approach to transfer capabilities from complex teacher models to smaller, resource-efficient student models that can be deployed easily, particularly in task-aware scenarios. However, existing methods of task-aware distillation typically require substantial quantities of data which may be unavailable or expensive to obtain in many practical scenarios. In this paper, we address this challenge by introducing a novel strategy called Counterfactual-explanation-infused Distillation CoD for few-shot task-aware knowledge distillation by systematically infusing counterfactual explanations. Counterfactual explanations (CFEs) refer to inputs that can flip the output prediction of the teacher model with minimum perturbation. Our strategy CoD leverages these CFEs to precisely map the teacher's decision boundary with significantly fewer samples. We provide theoretical guarantees for motivating the role of CFEs in distillation, from both statistical and geometric perspectives. We mathematically show that CFEs can improve parameter estimation by providing more informative examples near the teacher's decision boundary. We also derive geometric insights on how CFEs effectively act as knowledge probes, helping the students mimic the teacher's decision boundaries more effectively than standard data. We perform experiments across various datasets and LLMs to show that CoD outperforms standard distillation approaches in few-shot regimes (as low as 8-512 samples). Notably, CoD only uses half of the original samples used by the baselines, paired with their corresponding CFEs and still improves performance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21631","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:59:07Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:59:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:59:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21623","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21623","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21623","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiguang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Qiguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinhao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jinhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Libo","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Libo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yimeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yimeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihao","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Yihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shangxu","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Shangxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengyu","familyName":"Luan","name":"Luan, Chengyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dengyun","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Dengyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanjing","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hanjing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiannan","familyName":"Guan","name":"Guan, Jiannan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaqi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jiaqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengkang","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Mengkang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yantao","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Yantao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xie","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wanxiang","familyName":"Che","name":"Che, Wanxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Universal Landscape of Human Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T16:26:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T01:01:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Understanding how information is dynamically accumulated and transformed in human reasoning has long challenged cognitive psychology, philosophy, and artificial intelligence. Existing accounts, from classical logic to probabilistic models, illuminate aspects of output or individual modelling, but do not offer a unified, quantitative description of general human reasoning dynamics. To solve this, we introduce Information Flow Tracking (IF-Track), that uses large language models (LLMs) as probabilistic encoder to quantify information entropy and gain at each reasoning step. Through fine-grained analyses across diverse tasks, our method is the first successfully models the universal landscape of human reasoning behaviors within a single metric space. We show that IF-Track captures essential reasoning features, identifies systematic error patterns, and characterizes individual differences. Applied to discussion of advanced psychological theory, we first reconcile single- versus dual-process theories in IF-Track and discover the alignment of artificial and human cognition and how LLMs reshaping human reasoning process. This approach establishes a quantitative bridge between theory and measurement, offering mechanistic insights into the architecture of reasoning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21623","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:58:56Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:58:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:58:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21606","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21606","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21606","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxiang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiaxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawei","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Jiawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joey Tianyi","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Joey Tianyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingkun","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Mingkun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zuozhu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zuozhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Modest-Align: Data-Efficient Alignment for Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T16:11:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:59:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Cross-modal alignment aims to map heterogeneous modalities into a shared latent space, as exemplified by models like CLIP, which benefit from large-scale image-text pretraining for strong recognition capabilities. However, when operating in resource-constrained settings with limited or low-quality data, these models often suffer from overconfidence and degraded performance due to the prevalence of ambiguous or weakly correlated image-text pairs. Current contrastive learning approaches, which rely on single positive pairs, further exacerbate this issue by reinforcing overconfidence on uncertain samples. To address these challenges, we propose Modest-Align, a lightweight alignment framework designed for robustness and efficiency. Our approach leverages two complementary strategies -- Random Perturbation, which introduces controlled noise to simulate uncertainty, and Embedding Smoothing, which calibrates similarity distributions in the embedding space. These mechanisms collectively reduce overconfidence and improve performance on noisy or weakly aligned samples. Extensive experiments across multiple benchmark datasets demonstrate that Modest-Align outperforms state-of-the-art methods in retrieval tasks, achieving competitive results with over 100x less training data and 600x less GPU time than CLIP. Our method offers a practical and scalable solution for cross-modal alignment in real-world, low-resource scenarios."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21606","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:58:33Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:58:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:58:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21604","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21604","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21604","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xueyuan","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Xueyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cehao","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Cehao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ye","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Ye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ming","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rongjunchen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Rongjunchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Ni","name":"Ni, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaojun","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xiaojun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengjin","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Chengjin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RETuning: Upgrading Inference-Time Scaling for Stock Movement Prediction with Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T16:08:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:59:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recently, large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated outstanding reasoning capabilities on mathematical and coding tasks. However, their application to financial tasks-especially the most fundamental task of stock movement prediction-remains underexplored. We study a three-class classification problem (up, hold, down) and, by analyzing existing reasoning responses, observe that: (1) LLMs follow analysts' opinions rather than exhibit a systematic, independent analytical logic (CoTs). (2) LLMs list summaries from different sources without weighing adversarial evidence, yet such counterevidence is crucial for reliable prediction. It shows that the model does not make good use of its reasoning ability to complete the task. To address this, we propose Reflective Evidence Tuning (RETuning), a cold-start method prior to reinforcement learning, to enhance prediction ability. While generating CoT, RETuning encourages dynamically constructing an analytical framework from diverse information sources, organizing and scoring evidence for price up or down based on that framework-rather than on contextual viewpoints-and finally reflecting to derive the prediction. This approach maximally aligns the model with its learned analytical framework, ensuring independent logical reasoning and reducing undue influence from context. We also build a large-scale dataset spanning all of 2024 for 5,123 A-share stocks, with long contexts (32K tokens) and over 200K samples. In addition to price and news, it incorporates analysts' opinions, quantitative reports, fundamental data, macroeconomic indicators, and similar stocks. Experiments show that RETuning successfully unlocks the model's reasoning ability in the financial domain. Inference-time scaling still works even after 6 months or on out-of-distribution stocks, since the models gain valuable insights about stock movement prediction."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21604","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:58:30Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:58:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:58:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21603","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21603","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21603","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kuicai","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Kuicai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shurui","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Shurui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fangda","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Fangda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dexun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Dexun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Wenjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qu","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Qu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Gang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yichao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yichao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Doc-Researcher: A Unified System for Multimodal Document Parsing and Deep Research"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T16:07:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:59:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Deep Research systems have revolutionized how LLMs solve complex questions through iterative reasoning and evidence gathering. However, current systems remain fundamentally constrained to textual web data, overlooking the vast knowledge embedded in multimodal documents Processing such documents demands sophisticated parsing to preserve visual semantics (figures, tables, charts, and equations), intelligent chunking to maintain structural coherence, and adaptive retrieval across modalities, which are capabilities absent in existing systems. In response, we present Doc-Researcher, a unified system that bridges this gap through three integrated components: (i) deep multimodal parsing that preserves layout structure and visual semantics while creating multi-granular representations from chunk to document level, (ii) systematic retrieval architecture supporting text-only, vision-only, and hybrid paradigms with dynamic granularity selection, and (iii) iterative multi-agent workflows that decompose complex queries, progressively accumulate evidence, and synthesize comprehensive answers across documents and modalities. To enable rigorous evaluation, we introduce M4DocBench, the first benchmark for Multi-modal, Multi-hop, Multi-document, and Multi-turn deep research. Featuring 158 expert-annotated questions with complete evidence chains across 304 documents, M4DocBench tests capabilities that existing benchmarks cannot assess. Experiments demonstrate that Doc-Researcher achieves 50.6% accuracy, 3.4xbetter than state-of-the-art baselines, validating that effective document research requires not just better retrieval, but fundamentally deep parsing that preserve multimodal integrity and support iterative research. Our work establishes a new paradigm for conducting deep research on multimodal document collections."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"preprint"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21603","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:58:29Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:58:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:58:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21585","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21585","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21585","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yassine El","familyName":"Ouahidi","name":"Ouahidi, Yassine El","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonathan","familyName":"Lys","name":"Lys, Jonathan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philipp","familyName":"Thölke","name":"Thölke, Philipp","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicolas","familyName":"Farrugia","name":"Farrugia, Nicolas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bastien","familyName":"Pasdeloup","name":"Pasdeloup, Bastien","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vincent","familyName":"Gripon","name":"Gripon, Vincent","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karim","familyName":"Jerbi","name":"Jerbi, Karim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giulia","familyName":"Lioi","name":"Lioi, Giulia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"REVE: A Foundation Model for EEG -- Adapting to Any Setup with Large-Scale Pretraining on 25,000 Subjects"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Neurons and Cognition (q-bio.NC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T15:52:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:59:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Foundation models have transformed AI by reducing reliance on task-specific data through large-scale pretraining. While successful in language and vision, their adoption in EEG has lagged due to the heterogeneity of public datasets, which are collected under varying protocols, devices, and electrode configurations. Existing EEG foundation models struggle to generalize across these variations, often restricting pretraining to a single setup, resulting in suboptimal performance, in particular under linear probing. We present REVE (Representation for EEG with Versatile Embeddings), a pretrained model explicitly designed to generalize across diverse EEG signals. REVE introduces a novel 4D positional encoding scheme that enables it to process signals of arbitrary length and electrode arrangement. Using a masked autoencoding objective, we pretrain REVE on over 60,000 hours of EEG data from 92 datasets spanning 25,000 subjects, representing the largest EEG pretraining effort to date. REVE achieves state-of-the-art results on 10 downstream EEG tasks, including motor imagery classification, seizure detection, sleep staging, cognitive load estimation, and emotion recognition. With little to no fine-tuning, it demonstrates strong generalization, and nuanced spatio-temporal modeling. We release code, pretrained weights, and tutorials to support standardized EEG research and accelerate progress in clinical neuroscience."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Code available at: https://brain-bzh.github.io/reve/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21585","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:58:04Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:58:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:58:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21575","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21575","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21575","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mojca","familyName":"Brglez","name":"Brglez, Mojca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Špela","familyName":"Vintar","name":"Vintar, Špela","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Polyester Girlfriends to Blind Mice: Creating the First Pragmatics Understanding Benchmarks for Slovene"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T15:43:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:58:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models are demonstrating increasing capabilities, excelling at benchmarks once considered very difficult. As their capabilities grow, there is a need for more challenging evaluations that go beyond surface-level linguistic competence. Namely, language competence involves not only syntax and semantics but also pragmatics, i.e., understanding situational meaning as shaped by context as well as linguistic and cultural norms. To contribute to this line of research, we introduce SloPragEval and SloPragMega, the first pragmatics understanding benchmarks for Slovene that contain altogether 405 multiple-choice questions. We discuss the difficulties of translation, describe the campaign to establish a human baseline, and report pilot evaluations with LLMs. Our results indicate that current models have greatly improved in understanding nuanced language but may still fail to infer implied speaker meaning in non-literal utterances, especially those that are culture-specific. We also observe a significant gap between proprietary and open-source models. Finally, we argue that benchmarks targeting nuanced language understanding and knowledge of the target culture must be designed with care, preferably constructed from native data, and validated with human responses."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21575","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:57:51Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:57:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:57:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21571","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21571","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21571","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qixiu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Qixiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaobo","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Yaobo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lin","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Lin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengtang","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Chengtang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingqi","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Lingqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyuan","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Zhiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huizhi","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Huizhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sicheng","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Sicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizhong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yizhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lily","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Lily","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaolong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jiaolong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baining","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Baining","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Scalable Vision-Language-Action Model Pretraining for Robotic Manipulation with Real-Life Human Activity Videos"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T15:39:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:58:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper presents a novel approach for pretraining robotic manipulation Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models using a large corpus of unscripted real-life video recordings of human hand activities. Treating human hand as dexterous robot end-effector, we show that \"in-the-wild\" egocentric human videos without any annotations can be transformed into data formats fully aligned with existing robotic V-L-A training data in terms of task granularity and labels. This is achieved by the development of a fully-automated holistic human activity analysis approach for arbitrary human hand videos. This approach can generate atomic-level hand activity segments and their language descriptions, each accompanied with framewise 3D hand motion and camera motion. We process a large volume of egocentric videos and create a hand-VLA training dataset containing 1M episodes and 26M frames. This training data covers a wide range of objects and concepts, dexterous manipulation tasks, and environment variations in real life, vastly exceeding the coverage of existing robot data. We design a dexterous hand VLA model architecture and pretrain the model on this dataset. The model exhibits strong zero-shot capabilities on completely unseen real-world observations. Additionally, fine-tuning it on a small amount of real robot action data significantly improves task success rates and generalization to novel objects in real robotic experiments. We also demonstrate the appealing scaling behavior of the model's task performance with respect to pretraining data scale. We believe this work lays a solid foundation for scalable VLA pretraining, advancing robots toward truly generalizable embodied intelligence."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project page: https://microsoft.github.io/VITRA/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21571","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:57:45Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:57:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:57:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21561","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21561","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21561","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sandra","familyName":"Mitrović","name":"Mitrović, Sandra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Kletz","name":"Kletz, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ljiljana","familyName":"Dolamic","name":"Dolamic, Ljiljana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabio","familyName":"Rinaldi","name":"Rinaldi, Fabio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Are the LLMs Capable of Maintaining at Least the Language Genus?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T15:20:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:57:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) display notable variation in multilingual behavior, yet the role of genealogical language structure in shaping this variation remains underexplored. In this paper, we investigate whether LLMs exhibit sensitivity to linguistic genera by extending prior analyses on the MultiQ dataset. We first check if models prefer to switch to genealogically related languages when prompt language fidelity is not maintained. Next, we investigate whether knowledge consistency is better preserved within than across genera. We show that genus-level effects are present but strongly conditioned by training resource availability. We further observe distinct multilingual strategies across LLMs families. Our findings suggest that LLMs encode aspects of genus-level structure, but training data imbalances remain the primary factor shaping their multilingual performance."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21561","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:57:32Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:57:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:57:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21557","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21557","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21557","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongwei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hongwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ji","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Ji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiqing","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Shiqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenxiang","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Chenxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoran","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Haoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yurui","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yurui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongsheng","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Yongsheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanqin","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Yanqin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingjun","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Lingjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baoli","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Baoli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fang","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Fang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Co-Sight: Enhancing LLM-Based Agents via Conflict-Aware Meta-Verification and Trustworthy Reasoning with Structured Facts"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T15:14:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:57:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Long-horizon reasoning in LLM-based agents often fails not from generative weakness but from insufficient verification of intermediate reasoning. Co-Sight addresses this challenge by turning reasoning into a falsifiable and auditable process through two complementary mechanisms: Conflict-Aware Meta-Verification (CAMV) and Trustworthy Reasoning with Structured Facts (TRSF). CAMV reformulates verification as conflict identification and targeted falsification, allocating computation only to disagreement hotspots among expert agents rather than to full reasoning chains. This bounds verification cost to the number of inconsistencies and improves efficiency and reliability. TRSF continuously organizes, validates, and synchronizes evidence across agents through a structured facts module. By maintaining verified, traceable, and auditable knowledge, it ensures that all reasoning is grounded in consistent, source-verified information and supports transparent verification throughout the reasoning process. Together, TRSF and CAMV form a closed verification loop, where TRSF supplies structured facts and CAMV selectively falsifies or reinforces them, yielding transparent and trustworthy reasoning. Empirically, Co-Sight achieves state-of-the-art accuracy on GAIA (84.4%) and Humanity's Last Exam (35.5%), and strong results on Chinese-SimpleQA (93.8%). Ablation studies confirm that the synergy between structured factual grounding and conflict-aware verification drives these improvements. Co-Sight thus offers a scalable paradigm for reliable long-horizon reasoning in LLM-based agents. Code is available at https://github.com/ZTE-AICloud/Co-Sight/tree/cosight2.0_benchmarks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21557","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:57:26Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:57:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:57:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21551","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21551","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21551","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jialu","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Jialu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hung Manh","familyName":"Pham","name":"Pham, Hung Manh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ignace","familyName":"De Lathauwer","name":"De Lathauwer, Ignace","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Henk S.","familyName":"Schipper","name":"Schipper, Henk S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aaqib","familyName":"Saeed","name":"Saeed, Aaqib","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Interpretable Multimodal Zero-Shot ECG Diagnosis via Structured Clinical Knowledge Alignment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T15:09:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:56:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Electrocardiogram (ECG) interpretation is essential for cardiovascular disease diagnosis, but current automated systems often struggle with transparency and generalization to unseen conditions. To address this, we introduce ZETA, a zero-shot multimodal framework designed for interpretable ECG diagnosis aligned with clinical workflows. ZETA uniquely compares ECG signals against structured positive and negative clinical observations, which are curated through an LLM-assisted, expert-validated process, thereby mimicking differential diagnosis. Our approach leverages a pre-trained multimodal model to align ECG and text embeddings without disease-specific fine-tuning. Empirical evaluations demonstrate ZETA's competitive zero-shot classification performance and, importantly, provide qualitative and quantitative evidence of enhanced interpretability, grounding predictions in specific, clinically relevant positive and negative diagnostic features. ZETA underscores the potential of aligning ECG analysis with structured clinical knowledge for building more transparent, generalizable, and trustworthy AI diagnostic systems. We will release the curated observation dataset and code to facilitate future research."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21551","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:57:17Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:57:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:57:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21538","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21538","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21538","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Likun","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Likun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kuan-Wei","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Kuan-Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joy","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Joy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"InterpDetect: Interpretable Signals for Detecting Hallucinations in Retrieval-Augmented Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T15:02:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:56:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) integrates external knowledge to mitigate hallucinations, yet models often generate outputs inconsistent with retrieved content. Accurate hallucination detection requires disentangling the contributions of external context and parametric knowledge, which prior methods typically conflate. We investigate the mechanisms underlying RAG hallucinations and find they arise when later-layer FFN modules disproportionately inject parametric knowledge into the residual stream. To address this, we explore a mechanistic detection approach based on external context scores and parametric knowledge scores. Using Qwen3-0.6b, we compute these scores across layers and attention heads and train regression-based classifiers to predict hallucinations. Our method is evaluated against state-of-the-art LLMs (GPT-5, GPT-4.1) and detection baselines (RAGAS, TruLens, RefChecker). Furthermore, classifiers trained on Qwen3-0.6b signals generalize to GPT-4.1-mini responses, demonstrating the potential of proxy-model evaluation. Our results highlight mechanistic signals as efficient, generalizable predictors for hallucination detection in RAG systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21538","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:56:59Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:57:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:57:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21524","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21524","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21524","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ilija","familyName":"Lichkovski","name":"Lichkovski, Ilija","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Müller","name":"Müller, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mariam","familyName":"Ibrahim","name":"Ibrahim, Mariam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tiwai","familyName":"Mhundwa","name":"Mhundwa, Tiwai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EU-Agent-Bench: Measuring Illegal Behavior of LLM Agents Under EU Law"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T14:48:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:55:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed as agents in various contexts by providing tools at their disposal. However, LLM agents can exhibit unpredictable behaviors, including taking undesirable and/or unsafe actions. In order to measure the latent propensity of LLM agents for taking illegal actions under an EU legislative context, we introduce EU-Agent-Bench, a verifiable human-curated benchmark that evaluates an agent's alignment with EU legal norms in situations where benign user inputs could lead to unlawful actions. Our benchmark spans scenarios across several categories, including data protection, bias/discrimination, and scientific integrity, with each user request allowing for both compliant and non-compliant execution of the requested actions. Comparing the model's function calls against a rubric exhaustively supported by citations of the relevant legislature, we evaluate the legal compliance of frontier LLMs, and furthermore investigate the compliance effect of providing the relevant legislative excerpts in the agent's system prompt along with explicit instructions to comply. We release a public preview set for the research community, while holding out a private test set to prevent data contamination in evaluating upcoming models. We encourage future work extending agentic safety benchmarks to different legal jurisdictions and to multi-turn and multilingual interactions. We release our code on \\href{https://github.com/ilijalichkovski/eu-agent-bench}{this URL}."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at the Workshop on Regulatable ML at the 39th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2025)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21524","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:56:40Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:56:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:56:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21520","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21520","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21520","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Omer","familyName":"Moussa","name":"Moussa, Omer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mariya","familyName":"Toneva","name":"Toneva, Mariya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Brain-tuning Improves Generalizability and Efficiency of Brain Alignment in Speech Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T14:42:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:54:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Pretrained language models are remarkably effective in aligning with human brain responses elicited by natural language stimuli, positioning them as promising model organisms for studying language processing in the brain. However, existing approaches for both estimating and improving this brain alignment are participant-dependent and highly affected by the amount of data available per participant, hindering both generalization to new participants and population-level analyses. In this work, we address these limitations by introducing a scalable, generalizable brain-tuning method, in which we fine-tune pretrained speech language models to jointly predict fMRI responses from multiple participants. We demonstrate that the resulting brain-tuned models exhibit strong individual brain alignment while generalizing across participants. Specifically, our method leads to 1) a 5-fold decrease in the amount of fMRI data needed to predict brain data from new participants, 2) up to a 50% increase in the overall brain alignment, and 3) strong generalization to new unseen datasets. Furthermore, this multi-participant brain-tuning additionally improves downstream performance on semantic tasks, suggesting that training using brain data from multiple participants leads to more generalizable semantic representations. Taken together, these findings demonstrate a bidirectional benefit between neuroscience and AI, helping bridge the gap between the two fields. We make our code and models publicly available at https://github.com/bridge-ai-neuro/multi-brain-tuning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published at the 39th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2025)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21520","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:56:35Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:56:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:56:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21508","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21508","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21508","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Victor","familyName":"Jüttner","name":"Jüttner, Victor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Charlotte S.","familyName":"Löffler","name":"Löffler, Charlotte S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Erik","familyName":"Buchmann","name":"Buchmann, Erik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Actionable Cybersecurity Notifications for Smart Homes: A User Study on the Role of Length and Complexity"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T14:36:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:54:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1007/978-3-032-07989-3_19","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The proliferation of smart home devices has increased convenience but also introduced cybersecurity risks for everyday users, as many devices lack robust security features. Intrusion Detection Systems are a prominent approach to detecting cybersecurity threats. However, their alerts often use technical terms and require users to interpret them correctly, which is challenging for a typical smart home user. Large Language Models can bridge this gap by translating IDS alerts into actionable security notifications. However, it has not yet been clear what an actionable cybersecurity notification should look like. In this paper, we conduct an experimental online user study with 130 participants to examine how the length and complexity of LLM-generated notifications affect user likability, understandability, and motivation to act. Our results show that intermediate-complexity notifications are the most effective across all user groups, regardless of their technological proficiency. Across the board, users rated beginner-level messages as more effective when they were longer, while expert-level messages were rated marginally more effective when they were shorter. These findings provide insights for designing security notifications that are both actionable and broadly accessible to smart home users."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This version of the article has been accepted for publication, but is not the Version of Record and does not reflect post-acceptance improvements, or any corrections. The Version of Record is available online at: https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-032-07989-3_19"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21508","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-27T01:56:19Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:56:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:56:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21501","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21501","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21501","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanghao","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Guanghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowen","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Bowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingxing","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Mingxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruoyu","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Ruoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peisen","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Peisen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhibo","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhibo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenrui","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Wenrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junni","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Junni","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongkai","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Hongkai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaopeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiaopeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GranViT: A Fine-Grained Vision Model With Autoregressive Perception For MLLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T12:33:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:53:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision encoders are indispensable for allowing impressive performance of Multi-modal Large Language Models (MLLMs) in vision language tasks such as visual question answering and reasoning. However, existing vision encoders focus on global image representations but overlook fine-grained regional analysis. They are limited in fine grained perception due to the scarcity of fine grained annotated data and the lack of a fine grained pre-training paradigm. In this paper, we propose GranViT, a novel Vision Transformer that integrates fine-grained feature extraction with semantic alignment to Large Language Models (LLMs) via region level autoregressive training. We first construct Gran-29M, a dataset comprising 2million natural and OCR images paired with over 180 million high-quality region-level annotations, to enable large scale fine grained pretraining. Consequently, we develop a pretraining-adaptation framework along with a self distillation mechanism to train fine-grained GranViT on Gran-29M. We sufficiently exploit the fine-grained annotations from Gran-29M to resort to bounding-box-to-caption regression to enhance localized visual representation of the vision encoder in the pretraining and caption-to-bounding-box regression to improve vision feature utilization and localization for LLM in the adaptation. We further incorporate a self distillation mechanism that imposes explicit localization constraints on the vision encoder to strengthen its regional reasoning capability. Extensive experiments show that GranViT surpasses existing vision encoders and attains strong transferability to varying LLMs. Remarkably, it achieves state-of-the-art results on fine-grained recognition, multimodal VQA, and OCR understanding."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"21 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21501","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:56:10Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:56:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:56:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21479","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21479","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21479","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yating","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yating","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qijun","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Qijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lintao","familyName":"Xiang","name":"Xiang, Lintao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hujun","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Hujun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ITC-RWKV: Interactive Tissue-Cell Modeling with Recurrent Key-Value Aggregation for Histopathological Subtyping"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T14:03:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:52:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Accurate interpretation of histopathological images demands integration of information across spatial and semantic scales, from nuclear morphology and cellular textures to global tissue organization and disease-specific patterns. Although recent foundation models in pathology have shown strong capabilities in capturing global tissue context, their omission of cell-level feature modeling remains a key limitation for fine-grained tasks such as cancer subtype classification. To address this, we propose a dual-stream architecture that models the interplay between macroscale tissue features and aggregated cellular representations. To efficiently aggregate information from large cell sets, we propose a receptance-weighted key-value aggregation model, a recurrent transformer that captures inter-cell dependencies with linear complexity. Furthermore, we introduce a bidirectional tissue-cell interaction module to enable mutual attention between localized cellular cues and their surrounding tissue environment. Experiments on four histopathological subtype classification benchmarks show that the proposed method outperforms existing models, demonstrating the critical role of cell-level aggregation and tissue-cell interaction in fine-grained computational pathology."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accept by BMVC 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21479","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:55:40Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:55:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:55:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21473","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21473","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21473","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenglong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chenglong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Gan","name":"Gan, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hang","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Hang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chi","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Chi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongyu","familyName":"Mu","name":"Mu, Yongyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Murun","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Murun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Bei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunliang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chunliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tongran","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Tongran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingbo","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Jingbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengtao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Zhengtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Tong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MRO: Enhancing Reasoning in Diffusion Language Models via Multi-Reward Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T13:57:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:52:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in diffusion language models (DLMs) have presented a promising alternative to traditional autoregressive large language models (LLMs). However, DLMs still lag behind LLMs in reasoning performance, especially as the number of denoising steps decreases. Our analysis reveals that this shortcoming arises primarily from the independent generation of masked tokens across denoising steps, which fails to capture the token correlation. In this paper, we define two types of token correlation: intra-sequence correlation and inter-sequence correlation, and demonstrate that enhancing these correlations improves reasoning performance. To this end, we propose a Multi-Reward Optimization (MRO) approach, which encourages DLMs to consider the token correlation during the denoising process. More specifically, our MRO approach leverages test-time scaling, reject sampling, and reinforcement learning to directly optimize the token correlation with multiple elaborate rewards. Additionally, we introduce group step and importance sampling strategies to mitigate reward variance and enhance sampling efficiency. Through extensive experiments, we demonstrate that MRO not only improves reasoning performance but also achieves significant sampling speedups while maintaining high performance on reasoning benchmarks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21473","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:55:32Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:55:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:55:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21460","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21460","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21460","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sean","familyName":"McGregor","name":"McGregor, Sean","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Victor","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Victor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vassil","familyName":"Tashev","name":"Tashev, Vassil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Armstrong","familyName":"Foundjem","name":"Foundjem, Armstrong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aishwarya","familyName":"Ramasethu","name":"Ramasethu, Aishwarya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sadegh AlMahdi Kazemi","familyName":"Zarkouei","name":"Zarkouei, Sadegh AlMahdi Kazemi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chris","familyName":"Knotz","name":"Knotz, Chris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kongtao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Kongtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alicia","familyName":"Parrish","name":"Parrish, Alicia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anka","familyName":"Reuel","name":"Reuel, Anka","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heather","familyName":"Frase","name":"Frase, Heather","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Risk Management for Mitigating Benchmark Failure Modes: BenchRisk"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T13:43:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:51:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language model (LLM) benchmarks inform LLM use decisions (e.g., \"is this LLM safe to deploy for my use case and context?\"). However, benchmarks may be rendered unreliable by various failure modes that impact benchmark bias, variance, coverage, or people's capacity to understand benchmark evidence. Using the National Institute of Standards and Technology's risk management process as a foundation, this research iteratively analyzed 26 popular benchmarks, identifying 57 potential failure modes and 196 corresponding mitigation strategies. The mitigations reduce failure likelihood and/or severity, providing a frame for evaluating \"benchmark risk,\" which is scored to provide a metaevaluation benchmark: BenchRisk. Higher scores indicate that benchmark users are less likely to reach an incorrect or unsupported conclusion about an LLM. All 26 scored benchmarks present significant risk within one or more of the five scored dimensions (comprehensiveness, intelligibility, consistency, correctness, and longevity), which points to important open research directions for the field of LLM benchmarking. The BenchRisk workflow allows for comparison between benchmarks; as an open-source tool, it also facilitates the identification and sharing of risks and their mitigations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"19 pages, 7 figures, to be published in the 39th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2025)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21460","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:55:14Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:55:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:55:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21449","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21449","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21449","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengtian","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Shengtian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingshi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yingshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingrou","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jingrou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MoniTor: Exploiting Large Language Models with Instruction for Online Video Anomaly Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T13:28:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:50:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Video Anomaly Detection (VAD) aims to locate unusual activities or behaviors within videos. Recently, offline VAD has garnered substantial research attention, which has been invigorated by the progress in large language models (LLMs) and vision-language models (VLMs), offering the potential for a more nuanced understanding of anomalies. However, online VAD has seldom received attention due to real-time constraints and computational intensity. In this paper, we introduce a novel Memory-based online scoring queue scheme for Training-free VAD (MoniTor), to address the inherent complexities in online VAD. Specifically, MoniTor applies a streaming input to VLMs, leveraging the capabilities of pre-trained large-scale models. To capture temporal dependencies more effectively, we incorporate a novel prediction mechanism inspired by Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks. This ensures the model can effectively model past states and leverage previous predictions to identify anomalous behaviors. Thereby, it better understands the current frame. Moreover, we design a scoring queue and an anomaly prior to dynamically store recent scores and cover all anomalies in the monitoring scenario, providing guidance for LLMs to distinguish between normal and abnormal behaviors over time. We evaluate MoniTor on two large datasets (i.e., UCF-Crime and XD-Violence) containing various surveillance and real-world scenarios. The results demonstrate that MoniTor outperforms state-of-the-art methods and is competitive with weakly supervised methods without training. Code is available at https://github.com/YsTvT/MoniTor."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to NeurIPS 2025. The first two authors hold equal contributions"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21449","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:54:59Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:55:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:55:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21445","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21445","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21445","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thanh Cong","familyName":"Ho","name":"Ho, Thanh Cong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Farah","familyName":"Kharrat","name":"Kharrat, Farah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abderrazek","familyName":"Abid","name":"Abid, Abderrazek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fakhri","familyName":"Karray","name":"Karray, Fakhri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"REMONI: An Autonomous System Integrating Wearables and Multimodal Large Language Models for Enhanced Remote Health Monitoring"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T13:23:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:50:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/memea60663.2024.10596778","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the widespread adoption of wearable devices in our daily lives, the demand and appeal for remote patient monitoring have significantly increased. Most research in this field has concentrated on collecting sensor data, visualizing it, and analyzing it to detect anomalies in specific diseases such as diabetes, heart disease and depression. However, this domain has a notable gap in the aspect of human-machine interaction. This paper proposes REMONI, an autonomous REmote health MONItoring system that integrates multimodal large language models (MLLMs), the Internet of Things (IoT), and wearable devices. The system automatically and continuously collects vital signs, accelerometer data from a special wearable (such as a smartwatch), and visual data in patient video clips collected from cameras. This data is processed by an anomaly detection module, which includes a fall detection model and algorithms to identify and alert caregivers of the patient's emergency conditions. A distinctive feature of our proposed system is the natural language processing component, developed with MLLMs capable of detecting and recognizing a patient's activity and emotion while responding to healthcare worker's inquiries. Additionally, prompt engineering is employed to integrate all patient information seamlessly. As a result, doctors and nurses can access real-time vital signs and the patient's current state and mood by interacting with an intelligent agent through a user-friendly web application. Our experiments demonstrate that our system is implementable and scalable for real-life scenarios, potentially reducing the workload of medical professionals and healthcare costs. A full-fledged prototype illustrating the functionalities of the system has been developed and being tested to demonstrate the robustness of its various capabilities."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21445","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-27T01:54:53Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:54:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:54:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21443","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21443","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21443","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad Amin","familyName":"Zadenoori","name":"Zadenoori, Mohammad Amin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vincenzo","familyName":"De Martino","name":"De Martino, Vincenzo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jacek","familyName":"Dabrowski","name":"Dabrowski, Jacek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xavier","familyName":"Franch","name":"Franch, Xavier","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessio","familyName":"Ferrari","name":"Ferrari, Alessio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Does Model Size Matter? A Comparison of Small and Large Language Models for Requirements Classification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T13:20:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:50:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"[Context and motivation] Large language models (LLMs) show notable results in natural language processing (NLP) tasks for requirements engineering (RE). However, their use is compromised by high computational cost, data sharing risks, and dependence on external services. In contrast, small language models (SLMs) offer a lightweight, locally deployable alternative. [Question/problem] It remains unclear how well SLMs perform compared to LLMs in RE tasks in terms of accuracy. [Results] Our preliminary study compares eight models, including three LLMs and five SLMs, on requirements classification tasks using the PROMISE, PROMISE Reclass, and SecReq datasets. Our results show that although LLMs achieve an average F1 score of 2% higher than SLMs, this difference is not statistically significant. SLMs almost reach LLMs performance across all datasets and even outperform them in recall on the PROMISE Reclass dataset, despite being up to 300 times smaller. We also found that dataset characteristics play a more significant role in performance than model size. [Contribution] Our study contributes with evidence that SLMs are a valid alternative to LLMs for requirements classification, offering advantages in privacy, cost, and local deployability."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21443","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:54:51Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:54:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:54:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21440","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21440","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21440","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giovanni","familyName":"Trappolini","name":"Trappolini, Giovanni","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Florin","familyName":"Cuconasu","name":"Cuconasu, Florin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simone","familyName":"Filice","name":"Filice, Simone","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yoelle","familyName":"Maarek","name":"Maarek, Yoelle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabrizio","familyName":"Silvestri","name":"Silvestri, Fabrizio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Redefining Retrieval Evaluation in the Era of LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T13:17:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:49:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Traditional Information Retrieval (IR) metrics, such as nDCG, MAP, and MRR, assume that human users sequentially examine documents with diminishing attention to lower ranks. This assumption breaks down in Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) systems, where search results are consumed by Large Language Models (LLMs), which, unlike humans, process all retrieved documents as a whole rather than sequentially. Additionally, traditional IR metrics do not account for related but irrelevant documents that actively degrade generation quality, rather than merely being ignored. Due to these two major misalignments, namely human vs. machine position discount and human relevance vs. machine utility, classical IR metrics do not accurately predict RAG performance. We introduce a utility-based annotation schema that quantifies both the positive contribution of relevant passages and the negative impact of distracting ones. Building on this foundation, we propose UDCG (Utility and Distraction-aware Cumulative Gain), a metric using an LLM-oriented positional discount to directly optimize the correlation with the end-to-end answer accuracy. Experiments on five datasets and six LLMs demonstrate that UDCG improves correlation by up to 36% compared to traditional metrics. Our work provides a critical step toward aligning IR evaluation with LLM consumers and enables more reliable assessment of RAG components"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21440","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:54:47Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:54:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:54:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21436","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21436","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21436","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ankur","familyName":"Sinha","name":"Sinha, Ankur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shobhit","familyName":"Arora","name":"Arora, Shobhit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dhaval","familyName":"Pujara","name":"Pujara, Dhaval","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AutoOpt: A Dataset and a Unified Framework for Automating Optimization Problem Solving"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.4","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T13:14:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:49:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This study presents AutoOpt-11k, a unique image dataset of over 11,000 handwritten and printed mathematical optimization models corresponding to single-objective, multi-objective, multi-level, and stochastic optimization problems exhibiting various types of complexities such as non-linearity, non-convexity, non-differentiability, discontinuity, and high-dimensionality. The labels consist of the LaTeX representation for all the images and modeling language representation for a subset of images. The dataset is created by 25 experts following ethical data creation guidelines and verified in two-phases to avoid errors. Further, we develop AutoOpt framework, a machine learning based automated approach for solving optimization problems, where the user just needs to provide an image of the formulation and AutoOpt solves it efficiently without any further human intervention. AutoOpt framework consists of three Modules: (i) M1 (Image_to_Text)- a deep learning model performs the Mathematical Expression Recognition (MER) task to generate the LaTeX code corresponding to the optimization formulation in image; (ii) M2 (Text_to_Text)- a small-scale fine-tuned LLM generates the PYOMO script (optimization modeling language) from LaTeX code; (iii) M3 (Optimization)- a Bilevel Optimization based Decomposition (BOBD) method solves the optimization formulation described in the PYOMO script. We use AutoOpt-11k dataset for training and testing of deep learning models employed in AutoOpt. The deep learning model for MER task (M1) outperforms ChatGPT, Gemini and Nougat on BLEU score metric. BOBD method (M3), which is a hybrid approach, yields better results on complex test problems compared to common approaches, like interior-point algorithm and genetic algorithm."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2025, 28 pages, 11 figures, 11 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21436","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:54:40Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:54:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:54:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21425","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21425","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21425","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maneeha","familyName":"Rani","name":"Rani, Maneeha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bhupesh Kumar","familyName":"Mishra","name":"Mishra, Bhupesh Kumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dhavalkumar","familyName":"Thakker","name":"Thakker, Dhavalkumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Advancing Symbolic Integration in Large Language Models: Beyond Conventional Neurosymbolic AI"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T13:05:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:48:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LLMs have demonstrated highly effective learning, human-like response generation,and decision-making capabilities in high-risk sectors. However, these models remain black boxes because they struggle to ensure transparency in responses. The literature has explored numerous approaches to address transparency challenges in LLMs, including Neurosymbolic AI (NeSy AI). NeSy AI approaches were primarily developed for conventional neural networks and are not well-suited to the unique features of LLMs. Consequently, there is a limited systematic understanding of how symbolic AI can be effectively integrated into LLMs. This paper aims to address this gap by first reviewing established NeSy AI methods and then proposing a novel taxonomy of symbolic integration in LLMs, along with a roadmap to merge symbolic techniques with LLMs. The roadmap introduces a new categorisation framework across four dimensions by organising existing literature within these categories. These include symbolic integration across various stages of LLM, coupling mechanisms, architectural paradigms, as well as algorithmic and application-level perspectives. The paper thoroughly identifies current benchmarks, cutting-edge advancements, and critical gaps within the field to propose a roadmap for future research. By highlighting the latest developments and notable gaps in the literature, it offers practical insights for implementing frameworks for symbolic integration into LLMs to enhance transparency."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21425","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:54:25Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:54:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:54:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21408","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21408","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21408","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Camila","familyName":"Kolling","name":"Kolling, Camila","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vy Ai","familyName":"Vo","name":"Vo, Vy Ai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mariya","familyName":"Toneva","name":"Toneva, Mariya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Large Language Models as Model Organisms for Human Associative Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T12:52:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:47:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Associative learning--forming links between co-occurring items--is fundamental to human cognition, reshaping internal representations in complex ways. Testing hypotheses on how representational changes occur in biological systems is challenging, but large language models (LLMs) offer a scalable alternative. Building on LLMs' in-context learning, we adapt a cognitive neuroscience associative learning paradigm and investigate how representations evolve across six models. Our initial findings reveal a non-monotonic pattern consistent with the Non-Monotonic Plasticity Hypothesis, with moderately similar items differentiating after learning. Leveraging the controllability of LLMs, we further show that this differentiation is modulated by the overlap of associated items with the broader vocabulary--a factor we term vocabulary interference, capturing how new associations compete with prior knowledge. We find that higher vocabulary interference amplifies differentiation, suggesting that representational change is influenced by both item similarity and global competition. Our findings position LLMs not only as powerful tools for studying representational dynamics in human-like learning systems, but also as accessible and general computational models for generating new hypotheses about the principles underlying memory reorganization in the brain."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21408","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:54:01Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:54:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:54:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21407","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21407","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21407","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyungjun","familyName":"Min","name":"Min, Kyungjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyumin","familyName":"Cho","name":"Cho, Kyumin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junhwan","familyName":"Jang","name":"Jang, Junhwan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seokhyeong","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Seokhyeong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"REvolution: An Evolutionary Framework for RTL Generation driven by Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Neural and Evolutionary Computing (cs.NE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T12:50:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:47:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are used for Register-Transfer Level (RTL) code generation, but they face two main challenges: functional correctness and Power, Performance, and Area (PPA) optimization. Iterative, feedback-based methods partially address these, but they are limited to local search, hindering the discovery of a global optimum. This paper introduces REvolution, a framework that combines Evolutionary Computation (EC) with LLMs for automatic RTL generation and optimization. REvolution evolves a population of candidates in parallel, each defined by a design strategy, RTL implementation, and evaluation feedback. The framework includes a dual-population algorithm that divides candidates into Fail and Success groups for bug fixing and PPA optimization, respectively. An adaptive mechanism further improves search efficiency by dynamically adjusting the selection probability of each prompt strategy according to its success rate. Experiments on the VerilogEval and RTLLM benchmarks show that REvolution increased the initial pass rate of various LLMs by up to 24.0 percentage points. The DeepSeek-V3 model achieved a final pass rate of 95.5\\%, comparable to state-of-the-art results, without the need for separate training or domain-specific tools. Additionally, the generated RTL designs showed significant PPA improvements over reference designs. This work introduces a new RTL design approach by combining LLMs' generative capabilities with EC's broad search power, overcoming the local-search limitations of previous methods."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted for publication at the 2026 Asia and South Pacific Design Automation Conference (ASP-DAC)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21407","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:54:00Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:54:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:54:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21401","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21401","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21401","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mojtaba","familyName":"Eshghie","name":"Eshghie, Mojtaba","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gabriele","familyName":"Morello","name":"Morello, Gabriele","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matteo","familyName":"Lauretano","name":"Lauretano, Matteo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandre","familyName":"Bartel","name":"Bartel, Alexandre","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Monperrus","name":"Monperrus, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FLAMES: Fine-tuning LLMs to Synthesize Invariants for Smart Contract Security"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T12:44:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:47:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Smart contract vulnerabilities cost billions of dollars annually, yet existing automated analysis tools fail to generate deployable defenses. We present FLAMES, a novel automated approach that synthesizes executable runtime guards as Solidity \"require\" statements to harden smart contracts against exploits. Unlike prior work that relies on vulnerability labels, symbolic analysis, or natural language specifications, FLAMES employs domain-adapted large language models trained through fill-in-the-middle supervised fine-tuning on real-world invariants extracted from 514,506 verified contracts. Our extensive evaluation across three dimensions demonstrates FLAMES's effectiveness: (1) Compilation: FLAMES achieves 96.7% compilability for synthesized invariant (2) Semantic Quality: on a curated test set of 5,000 challenging invariants, FLAMES produces exact or semantically equivalent matches to ground truth in 44.5% of cases; (3) Exploit Mitigation: FLAMES prevents 22 out of 108 real exploits (20.4%) while preserving contract functionality, and (4) FLAMES successfully blocks the real-world APEMAGA incident by synthesizing a pre-condition that mitigates the attack. FLAMES establishes that domain-adapted LLMs can automatically generate production-ready security defenses for smart contracts without requiring vulnerability detection, formal specifications, or human intervention. We release our code, model weights, datasets, and evaluation infrastructure to enable reproducible research in this critical domain."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21401","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:53:51Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:53:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:53:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21398","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21398","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21398","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ravindra Aribowo","familyName":"Tarunokusumo","name":"Tarunokusumo, Ravindra Aribowo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rafael Fernandes","familyName":"Cunha","name":"Cunha, Rafael Fernandes","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Boosting Accuracy and Efficiency of Budget Forcing in LLMs via Reinforcement Learning for Mathematical Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T12:39:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:47:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Test-time scaling methods have seen a rapid increase in popularity for its computational efficiency and parameter-independent training to improve reasoning performance on Large Language Models. One such method is called budget forcing, a decoding intervention strategy which allocates extra compute budget for thinking and elicits the inherent self-correcting behavior of the model. However, this relies on supervised fine-tuning (SFT) on long-context reasoning traces which causes performance degradation on smaller models due to verbose responses. For this reason, we offer a framework integrating reinforcement learning (RL) to improve token efficiency and boost the performance of a 1.5B model for mathematical reasoning. We demonstrate this using only 1.5K training samples and found that our SFT+RL model performed better on the GSM8K dataset with varying compute budgets. Our main findings showed an overall higher accuracy while significantly reducing its token usage by over 40% compared to the SFT model, revealing how RL can recover the losses due to long-context training and altogether improving performance in mathematical reasoning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted to the European Conference on Artificial Intelligence (ECAI)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21398","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:53:47Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:53:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:53:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21370","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21370","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21370","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zain Ul Abideen","familyName":"Tariq","name":"Tariq, Zain Ul Abideen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mahmood","familyName":"Al-Zubaidi","name":"Al-Zubaidi, Mahmood","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Uzair","familyName":"Shah","name":"Shah, Uzair","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Agus","name":"Agus, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mowafa","familyName":"Househ","name":"Househ, Mowafa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HIKMA: Human-Inspired Knowledge by Machine Agents through a Multi-Agent Framework for Semi-Autonomous Scientific Conferences"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Digital Libraries (cs.DL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T11:52:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:44:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"HIKMA Semi-Autonomous Conference is the first experiment in reimagining scholarly communication through an end-to-end integration of artificial intelligence into the academic publishing and presentation pipeline. This paper presents the design, implementation, and evaluation of the HIKMA framework, which includes AI dataset curation, AI-based manuscript generation, AI-assisted peer review, AI-driven revision, AI conference presentation, and AI archival dissemination. By combining language models, structured research workflows, and domain safeguards, HIKMA shows how AI can support - not replace traditional scholarly practices while maintaining intellectual property protection, transparency, and integrity. The conference functions as a testbed and proof of concept, providing insights into the opportunities and challenges of AI-enabled scholarship. It also examines questions about AI authorship, accountability, and the role of human-AI collaboration in research."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21370","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:53:07Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:53:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:53:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21345","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21345","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21345","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aymane El","familyName":"Firdoussi","name":"Firdoussi, Aymane El","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"El Mahdi","familyName":"Chayti","name":"Chayti, El Mahdi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohamed El Amine","familyName":"Seddik","name":"Seddik, Mohamed El Amine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Jaggi","name":"Jaggi, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"$α$-LoRA: Effective Fine-Tuning via Base Model Rescaling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T11:19:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:43:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Fine-tuning has proven to be highly effective in adapting pre-trained models to perform better on new desired tasks with minimal data samples. Among the most widely used approaches are reparameterization methods, which update a target module by augmenting its frozen weight matrix with an additional trainable weight matrix. The most prominent example is Low Rank Adaption (LoRA), which gained significant attention in recent years. In this paper, we introduce a new class of reparameterization methods for transfer learning, designed to enhance the generalization ability of fine-tuned models. We establish the effectiveness of our approach in a high-dimensional binary classification setting using tools from Random Matrix Theory, and further validate our theoretical findings through more realistic experiments, such as fine-tuning LLMs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21345","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:52:32Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:52:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:52:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21329","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21329","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21329","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["School of Electrical and Computer Sciences, IIT Bhubaneswar, India"],"givenName":"Priyanshu","familyName":"Karmakar","name":"Karmakar, Priyanshu","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["School of Electrical and Computer Sciences, IIT Bhubaneswar, India"],"givenName":"Soumyabrata","familyName":"Chaudhuri","name":"Chaudhuri, Soumyabrata","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Microsoft, India"],"givenName":"Shubhojit","familyName":"Mallick","name":"Mallick, Shubhojit","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Microsoft, India"],"givenName":"Manish","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Manish","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["School of Electrical and Computer Sciences, IIT Bhubaneswar, India"],"givenName":"Abhik","familyName":"Jana","name":"Jana, Abhik","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["School of Electrical and Computer Sciences, IIT Bhubaneswar, India"],"givenName":"Shreya","familyName":"Ghosh","name":"Ghosh, Shreya","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TripTide: A Benchmark for Adaptive Travel Planning under Disruptions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T10:39:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:41:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent efforts like TripCraft and TravelPlanner have advanced the use of Large Language Models ( LLMs) for personalized, constraint aware travel itinerary generation. Yet, real travel often faces disruptions. To address this, we present TripTide, the first benchmark evaluating LLM's ability to revise itineraries under realistic disruptions. TripTide models key dimensions such as disruption severity and traveler tolerance, enabling nuanced assessment of LLM adaptability to events like flight cancellations, weather closures, or overbooked attractions. We conduct a threefold evaluation. First, we introduce automatic metrics including Preservation of Intent (how well the revised plan maintains feasibility and goals), Responsiveness (promptness and appropriateness of disruption handling), and Adaptability (semantic, spatial, and sequential divergence between original and revised plans). Second, we apply an LLM-as-a-judge approach to automatically assess revision quality. Third, we perform manual expert evaluation to verify whether revisions preserve semantic, spatial, sequential, and responsive aspects. Our experiments show that LLMs maintain strong sequential consistency and semantic stability, while spatial deviations are larger for shorter trips but decrease with longer ones, indicating that extended plans encourage better geographic coherence. However, disruption-handling ability declines as plan length increases, highlighting limits in LLM robustness. TripTide establishes a benchmark for evaluating adaptability, personalization, and resilience in LLM-based travel planning under real-world uncertainty."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 12 tables and 7 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21329","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:52:10Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:52:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:52:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21326","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21326","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21326","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gianluca","familyName":"Sperduti","name":"Sperduti, Gianluca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alejandro","familyName":"Moreo","name":"Moreo, Alejandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Typoglycemia under the Hood: Investigating Language Models' Understanding of Scrambled Words"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T10:36:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:41:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Research in linguistics has shown that humans can read words with internally scrambled letters, a phenomenon recently dubbed typoglycemia. Some specific NLP models have recently been proposed that similarly demonstrate robustness to such distortions by ignoring the internal order of characters by design. This raises a fundamental question: how can models perform well when many distinct words (e.g., form and from) collapse into identical representations under typoglycemia? Our work, focusing exclusively on the English language, seeks to shed light on the underlying aspects responsible for this robustness. We hypothesize that the main reasons have to do with the fact that (i) relatively few English words collapse under typoglycemia, and that (ii) collapsed words tend to occur in contexts so distinct that disambiguation becomes trivial. In our analysis, we (i) analyze the British National Corpus to quantify word collapse and ambiguity under typoglycemia, (ii) evaluate BERT's ability to disambiguate collapsing forms, and (iii) conduct a probing experiment by comparing variants of BERT trained from scratch on clean versus typoglycemic Wikipedia text; our results reveal that the performance degradation caused by scrambling is smaller than expected."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21326","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:52:06Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:52:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:52:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21323","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21323","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21323","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shufan","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Shufan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junshu","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Junshu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingming","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Qingming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuhui","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shuhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VL-SAE: Interpreting and Enhancing Vision-Language Alignment with a Unified Concept Set"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T10:29:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:40:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The alignment of vision-language representations endows current Vision-Language Models (VLMs) with strong multi-modal reasoning capabilities. However, the interpretability of the alignment component remains uninvestigated due to the difficulty in mapping the semantics of multi-modal representations into a unified concept set. To address this problem, we propose VL-SAE, a sparse autoencoder that encodes vision-language representations into its hidden activations. Each neuron in its hidden layer correlates to a concept represented by semantically similar images and texts, thereby interpreting these representations with a unified concept set. To establish the neuron-concept correlation, we encourage semantically similar representations to exhibit consistent neuron activations during self-supervised training. First, to measure the semantic similarity of multi-modal representations, we perform their alignment in an explicit form based on cosine similarity. Second, we construct the VL-SAE with a distance-based encoder and two modality-specific decoders to ensure the activation consistency of semantically similar representations. Experiments across multiple VLMs (e.g., CLIP, LLaVA) demonstrate the superior capability of VL-SAE in interpreting and enhancing the vision-language alignment. For interpretation, the alignment between vision and language representations can be understood by comparing their semantics with concepts. For enhancement, the alignment can be strengthened by aligning vision-language representations at the concept level, contributing to performance improvements in downstream tasks, including zero-shot image classification and hallucination elimination. Codes are available at https://github.com/ssfgunner/VL-SAE."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21323","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:52:02Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:52:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:52:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21322","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21322","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21322","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antoine","familyName":"Boutet","name":"Boutet, Antoine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lucas","familyName":"Magnana","name":"Magnana, Lucas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Leverage Unlearning to Sanitize LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T10:28:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:40:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Pre-trained large language models (LLMs) are becoming useful for various tasks. To improve their performance on certain tasks, it is necessary to fine-tune them on specific data corpora (e.g., medical reports, business data). These specialized data corpora may contain sensitive data (e.g., personal or confidential data) that will be memorized by the model and likely to be regurgitated during its subsequent use. This memorization of sensitive information by the model poses a significant privacy or confidentiality issue. To remove this memorization and sanitize the model without requiring costly additional fine-tuning on a secured data corpus, we propose SANI. SANI is an unlearning approach to sanitize language models. It relies on both an erasure and repair phases that 1) reset certain neurons in the last layers of the model to disrupt the memorization of fine-grained information, and then 2) fine-tune the model while avoiding memorizing sensitive information. We comprehensively evaluate SANI to sanitize both a model fine-tuned and specialized with medical data by removing directly and indirectly identifiers from the memorization of the model, and a standard pre-trained model by removing specific terms defined as confidential information from the model. Results show that with only few additional epochs of unlearning, the model is sanitized and the number of regurgitations is drastically reduced. This approach can be particularly useful for hospitals or other industries that have already spent significant resources training models on large datasets and wish to sanitize them before sharing."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21322","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:52:00Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:52:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:52:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21317","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21317","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21317","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Danilo","familyName":"de Oliveira","name":"de Oliveira, Danilo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tal","familyName":"Peer","name":"Peer, Tal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonas","familyName":"Rochdi","name":"Rochdi, Jonas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Timo","familyName":"Gerkmann","name":"Gerkmann, Timo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Are These Even Words? Quantifying the Gibberishness of Generative Speech Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T10:22:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:40:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Significant research efforts are currently being dedicated to non-intrusive quality and intelligibility assessment, especially given how it enables curation of large scale datasets of in-the-wild speech data. However, with the increasing capabilities of generative models to synthesize high quality speech, new types of artifacts become relevant, such as generative hallucinations. While intrusive metrics are able to spot such sort of discrepancies from a reference signal, it is not clear how current non-intrusive methods react to high-quality phoneme confusions or, more extremely, gibberish speech. In this paper we explore how to factor in this aspect under a fully unsupervised setting by leveraging language models. Additionally, we publish a dataset of high-quality synthesized gibberish speech for further development of measures to assess implausible sentences in spoken language, alongside code for calculating scores from a variety of speech language models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21317","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:51:53Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:51:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:51:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21311","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21311","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21311","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Lu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiazuo","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Jiazuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haomiao","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Haomiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ping","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Ping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunzhi","familyName":"Zhuge","name":"Zhuge, Yunzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huchuan","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Huchuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"You","familyName":"He","name":"He, You","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FineRS: Fine-grained Reasoning and Segmentation of Small Objects with Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T10:14:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:39:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-modal Large Language Models (MLLMs) have shown remarkable capabilities across a wide range of vision-language tasks. However, due to the restricted input resolutions, MLLMs face significant challenges in precisely understanding and localizing visual details in high-resolution images -- particularly when dealing with extra-small objects embedded in cluttered contexts. To address this issue, we propose \\textsc{FineRS}, a two-stage MLLM-based reinforcement learning framework for jointly reasoning and segmenting extremely small objects within high-resolution scenes. \\textsc{FineRS} adopts a coarse-to-fine pipeline comprising Global Semantic Exploration (GSE) and Localized Perceptual Refinement (LPR). Specifically, GSE performs instruction-guided reasoning to generate a textural response and a coarse target region, while LPR refines this region to produce an accurate bounding box and segmentation mask. To couple the two stages, we introduce a locate-informed retrospective reward, where LPR's outputs are used to optimize GSE for more robust coarse region exploration. % Additionally, we present \\textsc{FineRS}-4k, a new dataset for evaluating MLLMs on attribute-level reasoning and pixel-level segmentation on subtle, small-scale targets in complex high-resolution scenes. Experimental results on \\textsc{FineRS}-4k and public datasets demonstrate that our method consistently outperforms state-of-the-art MLLM-based approaches on both instruction-guided segmentation and visual reasoning tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21311","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:51:44Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:51:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:51:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21302","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21302","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21302","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanghyun","familyName":"Ahn","name":"Ahn, Sanghyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wonje","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Wonje","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junyong","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Junyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinwoo","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Jinwoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Honguk","familyName":"Woo","name":"Woo, Honguk","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Reliable Code-as-Policies: A Neuro-Symbolic Framework for Embodied Task Planning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T10:01:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:39:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have enabled the automatic generation of executable code for task planning and control in embodied agents such as robots, demonstrating the potential of LLM-based embodied intelligence. However, these LLM-based code-as-policies approaches often suffer from limited environmental grounding, particularly in dynamic or partially observable settings, leading to suboptimal task success rates due to incorrect or incomplete code generation. In this work, we propose a neuro-symbolic embodied task planning framework that incorporates explicit symbolic verification and interactive validation processes during code generation. In the validation phase, the framework generates exploratory code that actively interacts with the environment to acquire missing observations while preserving task-relevant states. This integrated process enhances the grounding of generated code, resulting in improved task reliability and success rates in complex environments. We evaluate our framework on RLBench and in real-world settings across dynamic, partially observable scenarios. Experimental results demonstrate that our framework improves task success rates by 46.2% over Code-as-Policies baselines and attains over 86.8% executability of task-relevant actions, thereby enhancing the reliability of task planning in dynamic environments."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at NeurIPS 2025 Spotlight"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21302","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:51:31Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:51:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:51:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21258","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21258","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21258","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kumiko","familyName":"Tanaka-Ishii","name":"Tanaka-Ishii, Kumiko","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Correlation Dimension of Auto-Regressive Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Chaotic Dynamics (nlin.CD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T08:42:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:35:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have achieved remarkable progress in natural language generation, yet they continue to display puzzling behaviors -- such as repetition and incoherence -- even when exhibiting low perplexity. This highlights a key limitation of conventional evaluation metrics, which emphasize local prediction accuracy while overlooking long-range structural complexity. We introduce correlation dimension, a fractal-geometric measure of self-similarity, to quantify the epistemological complexity of text as perceived by a language model. This measure captures the hierarchical recurrence structure of language, bridging local and global properties in a unified framework. Through extensive experiments, we show that correlation dimension (1) reveals three distinct phases during pretraining, (2) reflects context-dependent complexity, (3) indicates a model's tendency toward hallucination, and (4) reliably detects multiple forms of degeneration in generated text. The method is computationally efficient, robust to model quantization (down to 4-bit precision), broadly applicable across autoregressive architectures (e.g., Transformer and Mamba), and provides fresh insight into the generative dynamics of LLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21258","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:50:29Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:50:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:50:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21228","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21228","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21228","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huizi","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Huizi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenkong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wenkong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiran","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yiran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayan","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jiayan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenyue","familyName":"Hua","name":"Hua, Wenyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinxin","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Xinxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjia","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Wenjia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lexuan","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Lexuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingyi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Bingyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Guang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ming-Li","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ming-Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Themistocles L.","familyName":"Assimes","name":"Assimes, Themistocles L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongfeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yongfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingyun","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Qingyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingyao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Lingyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lizhou","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Lizhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DispatchMAS: Fusing taxonomy and artificial intelligence agents for emergency medical services"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; J.3","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T07, 92C50","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T08:01:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:32:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Objective: Emergency medical dispatch (EMD) is a high-stakes process challenged by caller distress, ambiguity, and cognitive load. Large Language Models (LLMs) and Multi-Agent Systems (MAS) offer opportunities to augment dispatchers. This study aimed to develop and evaluate a taxonomy-grounded, LLM-powered multi-agent system for simulating realistic EMD scenarios. Methods: We constructed a clinical taxonomy (32 chief complaints, 6 caller identities from MIMIC-III) and a six-phase call protocol. Using this framework, we developed an AutoGen-based MAS with Caller and Dispatcher Agents. The system grounds interactions in a fact commons to ensure clinical plausibility and mitigate misinformation. We used a hybrid evaluation framework: four physicians assessed 100 simulated cases for \"Guidance Efficacy\" and \"Dispatch Effectiveness,\" supplemented by automated linguistic analysis (sentiment, readability, politeness). Results: Human evaluation, with substantial inter-rater agreement (Gwe's AC1 \u0026gt; 0.70), confirmed the system's high performance. It demonstrated excellent Dispatch Effectiveness (e.g., 94 % contacting the correct potential other agents) and Guidance Efficacy (advice provided in 91 % of cases), both rated highly by physicians. Algorithmic metrics corroborated these findings, indicating a predominantly neutral affective profile (73.7 % neutral sentiment; 90.4 % neutral emotion), high readability (Flesch 80.9), and a consistently polite style (60.0 % polite; 0 % impolite). Conclusion: Our taxonomy-grounded MAS simulates diverse, clinically plausible dispatch scenarios with high fidelity. Findings support its use for dispatcher training, protocol evaluation, and as a foundation for real-time decision support. This work outlines a pathway for safely integrating advanced AI agents into emergency response workflows."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"27 pages, 7 figures, 3 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21228","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:49:47Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:49:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:49:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21223","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21223","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21223","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kexuan","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Kexuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yandong","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Yandong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiyang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Weiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Model Merging with Functional Dual Anchors"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T07:54:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:32:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Model merging is an efficient post-training strategy for integrating knowledge from multiple finetuned checkpoints of a shared foundation model. Existing methods operate in the parameter space, combining task vectors to mitigate conflicts, but remain constrained by parameter inconsistencies. We propose Functional Dual Anchors (FDAs), a framework that instead models the input-representation space. FDAs are synthetic inputs whose induced gradients align with task vectors, capturing task-specific functional shifts relative to the pretrained model. This perspective bridges joint multi-task training and post-hoc merging, offering both robustness and flexibility. We further introduce a principled initialization scheme and show that FDAs are complementary to parameter-space model merging. Comprehensive experiments demonstrate the effectiveness of FDAs in model merging."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Technical report (23 pages, 15 figures, project page: https://spherelab.ai/fda/)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21223","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:49:39Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:49:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:49:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21214","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21214","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21214","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingwei","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Xingwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kar Wai","familyName":"Fok","name":"Fok, Kar Wai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vrizlynn L. L.","familyName":"Thing","name":"Thing, Vrizlynn L. L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Enhanced MLLM Black-Box Jailbreaking Attacks and Defenses"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T07:35:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:31:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) comprise of both visual and textual modalities to process vision language tasks. However, MLLMs are vulnerable to security-related issues, such as jailbreak attacks that alter the model's input to induce unauthorized or harmful responses. The incorporation of the additional visual modality introduces new dimensions to security threats. In this paper, we proposed a black-box jailbreak method via both text and image prompts to evaluate MLLMs. In particular, we designed text prompts with provocative instructions, along with image prompts that introduced mutation and multi-image capabilities. To strengthen the evaluation, we also designed a Re-attack strategy. Empirical results show that our proposed work can improve capabilities to assess the security of both open-source and closed-source MLLMs. With that, we identified gaps in existing defense methods to propose new strategies for both training-time and inference-time defense methods, and evaluated them across the new jailbreak methods. The experiment results showed that the re-designed defense methods improved protections against the jailbreak attacks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21214","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:49:26Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:49:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:49:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21209","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21209","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21209","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zixiang","familyName":"Wan","name":"Wan, Zixiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guochang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Guochang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifeng","familyName":"He","name":"He, Yifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianqiang","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Jianqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SpecTokenizer: A Lightweight Streaming Codec in the Compressed Spectrum Domain"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T07:25:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:30:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.21437/interspeech.2025-546","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Neural Audio Codecs (NACs) have gained growing attention in recent years as technologies for audio compression and audio representation in speech language models. While mainstream NACs typically require G-level computation and M-level parameters, the performance of lightweight and streaming NACs remains underexplored. This paper proposes SpecTokenizer, a lightweight streaming codec that operates in the compressed spectral domain. Composed solely of alternating CNN and RNN layers, SpecTokenizer achieves greater efficiency and better representational capability through multi-scale modeling in the compressed spectrum domain. At 4 kbps, the proposed SpecTokenizer achieves comparable or superior performance compared to the codec with state-of-the-art lightweight architecture while requiring only 20% of the computation and 10% of the parameters. Furthermore, it significantly outperforms the codec when using similar computational and storage resources."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by Interspeech 2025; 5 pages, 1 figure, 5 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21209","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-27T01:49:19Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:49:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:49:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21204","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21204","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21204","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiyuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Danielle C.","familyName":"Maddix","name":"Maddix, Danielle C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junming","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Junming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nick","familyName":"Erickson","name":"Erickson, Nick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdul Fatir","familyName":"Ansari","name":"Ansari, Abdul Fatir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boran","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Boran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuai","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leman","familyName":"Akoglu","name":"Akoglu, Leman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christos","familyName":"Faloutsos","name":"Faloutsos, Christos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael W.","familyName":"Mahoney","name":"Mahoney, Michael W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cuixiong","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Cuixiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huzefa","familyName":"Rangwala","name":"Rangwala, Huzefa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"George","familyName":"Karypis","name":"Karypis, George","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bernie","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Bernie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mitra: Mixed Synthetic Priors for Enhancing Tabular Foundation Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T07:15:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:30:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Since the seminal work of TabPFN, research on tabular foundation models (TFMs) based on in-context learning (ICL) has challenged long-standing paradigms in machine learning. Without seeing any real-world data, models pretrained on purely synthetic datasets generalize remarkably well across diverse datasets, often using only a moderate number of in-context examples. This shifts the focus in tabular machine learning from model architecture design to the design of synthetic datasets, or, more precisely, to the prior distributions that generate them. Yet the guiding principles for prior design remain poorly understood. This work marks the first attempt to address the gap. We systematically investigate and identify key properties of synthetic priors that allow pretrained TFMs to generalize well. Based on these insights, we introduce Mitra, a TFM trained on a curated mixture of synthetic priors selected for their diversity, distinctiveness, and performance on real-world tabular data. Mitra consistently outperforms state-of-the-art TFMs, such as TabPFNv2 and TabICL, across both classification and regression benchmarks, with better sample efficiency."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2025. We released both classifier (autogluon/mitra-classifier) and regressor (autogluon/mitra-regressor) model weights on HuggingFace"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21204","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:49:12Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:49:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:49:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21192","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21192","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21192","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luca","familyName":"Demetrio","name":"Demetrio, Luca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giovanni","familyName":"Apruzzese","name":"Apruzzese, Giovanni","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kathrin","familyName":"Grosse","name":"Grosse, Kathrin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pavel","familyName":"Laskov","name":"Laskov, Pavel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emil","familyName":"Lupu","name":"Lupu, Emil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vera","familyName":"Rimmer","name":"Rimmer, Vera","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philine","familyName":"Widmer","name":"Widmer, Philine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Gen-Review: A Large-scale Dataset of AI-Generated (and Human-written) Peer Reviews"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T06:54:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:29:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"How does the progressive embracement of Large Language Models (LLMs) affect scientific peer reviewing? This multifaceted question is fundamental to the effectiveness -- as well as to the integrity -- of the scientific process. Recent evidence suggests that LLMs may have already been tacitly used in peer reviewing, e.g., at the 2024 International Conference of Learning Representations (ICLR). Furthermore, some efforts have been undertaken in an attempt to explicitly integrate LLMs in peer reviewing by various editorial boards (including that of ICLR'25). To fully understand the utility and the implications of LLMs' deployment for scientific reviewing, a comprehensive relevant dataset is strongly desirable. Despite some previous research on this topic, such dataset has been lacking so far. We fill in this gap by presenting GenReview, the hitherto largest dataset containing LLM-written reviews. Our dataset includes 81K reviews generated for all submissions to the 2018--2025 editions of the ICLR by providing the LLM with three independent prompts: a negative, a positive, and a neutral one. GenReview is also linked to the respective papers and their original reviews, thereby enabling a broad range of investigations. To illustrate the value of GenReview, we explore a sample of intriguing research questions, namely: if LLMs exhibit bias in reviewing (they do); if LLM-written reviews can be automatically detected (so far, they can); if LLMs can rigorously follow reviewing instructions (not always) and whether LLM-provided ratings align with decisions on paper acceptance or rejection (holds true only for accepted papers). GenReview can be accessed at the following link: https://anonymous.4open.science/r/gen_review."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21192","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:48:55Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:48:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:48:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21189","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21189","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21189","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yukun","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Yukun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingjie","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Mingjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Backes","name":"Backes, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Adjacent Words, Divergent Intents: Jailbreaking Large Language Models via Task Concurrency"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T06:39:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:28:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite their superior performance on a wide range of domains, large language models (LLMs) remain vulnerable to misuse for generating harmful content, a risk that has been further amplified by various jailbreak attacks. Existing jailbreak attacks mainly follow sequential logic, where LLMs understand and answer each given task one by one. However, concurrency, a natural extension of the sequential scenario, has been largely overlooked. In this work, we first propose a word-level method to enable task concurrency in LLMs, where adjacent words encode divergent intents. Although LLMs maintain strong utility in answering concurrent tasks, which is demonstrated by our evaluations on mathematical and general question-answering benchmarks, we notably observe that combining a harmful task with a benign one significantly reduces the probability of it being filtered by the guardrail, showing the potential risks associated with concurrency in LLMs. Based on these findings, we introduce $\\texttt{JAIL-CON}$, an iterative attack framework that $\\underline{\\text{JAIL}}$breaks LLMs via task $\\underline{\\text{CON}}$currency. Experiments on widely-used LLMs demonstrate the strong jailbreak capabilities of $\\texttt{JAIL-CON}$ compared to existing attacks. Furthermore, when the guardrail is applied as a defense, compared to the sequential answers generated by previous attacks, the concurrent answers in our $\\texttt{JAIL-CON}$ exhibit greater stealthiness and are less detectable by the guardrail, highlighting the unique feature of task concurrency in jailbreaking LLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted in NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21189","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-27T01:48:50Z","registered":"2025-10-27T01:48:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-27T01:48:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":140,"totalPages":2,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=created%3A%5B2025-10-26T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-10-27T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D+AND+%28titles.title%3A%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+%22foundation+model%22+OR+%22foundation+models%22%29+OR+descriptions.description%3A%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+%22foundation+model%22+OR+%22foundation+models%22%29%29\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100","next":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=2\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026query=created%3A%5B2025-10-26T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-10-27T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D+AND+%28titles.title%3A%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+%22foundation+model%22+OR+%22foundation+models%22%29+OR+descriptions.description%3A%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+%22foundation+model%22+OR+%22foundation+models%22%29%29"}}